{"code":"AFRST 102","title":"Blackness Across Diaspora","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An interdisciplinary examination of the complex array of Black political, social and cultural practices across the Diaspora. It focuses on the historical and contemporary experiences of people of African descent in North and South America, the Caribbean, Latin America and Africa. The class will engage in close readings of classic texts and other readings from a variety of historical, literary, and artistic sources that offer insight into the dynamics of Black thought and practice and introduce core elements of the discipline. Open to first-years and sophomores only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-102","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"105","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nigel D. Westmaas"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 131W","title":"Representation Culture Power","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Forefronts the consequences of assumptions based on hegemonic ideas and representations of blackness and Black life. We examine how visual and narrative representations impact our perceptions and sense of collective and individual selves, and the lived experiences of Black subjects. We explore dominant cultural representations of blackness and how they are in dialogue, negotiation, and contestation, along with the tension and interaction between ideas inherited from the outside, and the created inner ones.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-131","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"210","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"210","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"210","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Merrill"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 151W","title":"Fiction and Autobiography","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"To study African American literature is to contend with the complexity of identity and belonging-How do we understand, or not, a unitary racial identity? Who are the \"We\" that cohere identity? What is the collective, and what is the personal? Our study explores how mid-to-late 20th-century writers advance and rewrite the boundaries of genre, questioning, for example, the relationship between fiction and autobiography. This course explores the innovative ways that black writers have reimagined their places in the nation and responded to the efficacy of identity, of race, gender, and sexuality, as ways to understand the self and the collective. Texts will include Audre Lorde's Zami, Jamaica Kincaid's Lucy, Rita Dove's Thomas and Beulah, selections from Essex Hemphill, and more.","cross_listed":["LIT 151W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-151","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"225","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years Only (FYC)","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"R","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 159","title":"Elementary Jazz Dance","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An introductory technique course exploring jazz dance as a form grounded in Black American cultural and performance traditions. Students develop foundational skills in rhythm, coordination, and jazz vocabulary while situating studio practice within the historical evolution of jazz dance. Attention is given to stylistic nuance, musicality, and expressive range within jazz movement. Open to all students; no previous dance experience required.","cross_listed":["DANCE 159"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-159","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Blood Fitness Center","capacity":25,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Blood Fitness Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"R","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Blood Fitness Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Nikolas C. Owens"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 161","title":"Jazz Foundations","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"A survey of jazz history from its roots in West African culture to the present day. Provides an analysis of jazz styles (i.e., blues, ragtime, New Orleans Jazz, swing, cool jazz, bebop, hard bop, modal jazz, free jazz) and a brief overview of the lives and music of influential jazz artists including Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Billie Holiday, Mary Lou Williams, Charlie Parker, Miles Davis, John Coltrane, Marian McPartland, Wynton Marsalis, and Ingrid Jensen. Includes a study of the social and the political context of the music and the way jazz has interfaced with social movements over the course of the 20th and 21st centuries. Students will analyze power structures based on race and gender, and their consequences in the jazz field. Not open to students who have taken MUSIC/AFRST 160/260 or MUSIC/AFRST 259/359.","cross_listed":["AFRST 261","MUSIC 161","MUSIC 261"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 190W","title":"Exploring Social Justice","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the historical movements for social justice and contributions of a new generation of black leadership including students, women and community organizers during the civil rights and Black power movements. We will consider the contributions of well-known figures like Huey Newton and Malcolm X and lesser-known figures like Septima Clark, the director of the freedom schools, and their significance in the present times.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-190","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"209","types":["Diversity Course","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"209","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"209","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Donald M. Carter"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 212","title":"Reading Blackness","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Blackness is a wide-ranging conversation, a highly debated idea in culture and in scholarship. This course introduces students to a survey of academic concerns surrounding Blackness in literature and various interpretive fields. Students will follow three major thinkers, W.E.B. Du Bois, Hortense Spillers, and Frantz Fanon, and their theories of Blackness as consciousness, as text, and as surface. In addition, students will apply their approaches to two novels by Toni Morrison and Ralph Ellison. By investigating how Blackness gets written and conceived as a concept in critical writing and fiction, students will develop a more robust understanding of race and how to apply it in academic discourse. In addition, this class introduces students to reading theory and peer-reviewed writing, where they will learn how to break down arguments and identify lines of inquiry. (History)","cross_listed":["LIT 212"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-212","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"227","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"227","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"227","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 215","title":"Global Race and Sport","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"The course is designed to examine race and diversity issues in the world of sports from the early 20th century to the present. Topics will examine and provide critical inquiry on the impact of race and racism in major world sports and the Olympic movement, including football(soccer), tennis, boxing, cricket, baseball, American football, and athletics. The course is inter-sectional in scope and interrogates issues of masculinity, gender, the structures of power, as well as new forms of global capitalism in sports, and individuals that have personified their areas of sporting achievement.","cross_listed":["SOC 215"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-215","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"305","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores, & Juniors only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Nigel D. Westmaas"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 220W","title":"Imagining Africa","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"The idea of Africa historically has served as a metaphor for exoticism, sexuality or savagery in western discourse. In the contemporary world, it has been imagined as the site of seemingly insoluble problems such as the collapse of the state, genocide & famine. Against this backdrop, the course explores colonial/neocolonial state structural and institutional hierarchies, related to the role of ideas of race, gender & class based inequalities in postcolonial societies, warfare, international humanitarian efforts and asymmetrical relationships between cultural traditions in Africa and the West.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-220","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"209","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"209","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"209","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Donald M. Carter"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 257","title":"Music & the Black Arts Mvmnt","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An examination of the function of music in the Black Arts Movement in the 1960s and 1970s, analyzing how music paralleled literature, drama, and visual art. The course draws connections between earlier forms of Afro-modernism in the Harlem Renaissance and demonstrates how musicians engaged in social activism by protesting systemic racism and white supremacy. The course considers Larry Neal's theory of the Black Aesthetic and Robert L. Douglas' theory of multidominant elements as lenses for interdisciplinary analysis. Includes a study of musicians who engaged in political activism during the Black Arts Movement and the Movement's influence on early hip-hop artists such as Public Enemy and Poor Righteous Teachers.","cross_listed":["AFRST 357","MUSIC 257","MUSIC 357"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 261","title":"Jazz Foundations","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"A survey of jazz history from its roots in West African culture to the present day. Provides an analysis of jazz styles (i.e., blues, ragtime, New Orleans Jazz, swing, cool jazz, bebop, hard bop, modal jazz, free jazz) and a brief overview of the lives and music of influential jazz artists including Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Billie Holiday, Mary Lou Williams, Charlie Parker, Miles Davis, John Coltrane, Marian McPartland, Wynton Marsalis, and Ingrid Jensen. Includes a study of the social and the political context of the music and the way jazz has interfaced with social movements over the course of the 20th and 21st centuries. Students will analyze power structures based on race and gender, and their consequences in the jazz field. Not open to students who have taken MUSIC/AFRST 160/260 or MUSIC/AFRST 259/359.","cross_listed":["AFRST 161","MUSIC 161","MUSIC 261"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 264","title":"Jazz & Black Popular Dance","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of the history North American Black vernacular dance forms with a predominant emphasis on jazz dance history as their progenitor. Exploration, investigation, and analysis of social, cultural, and aesthetic concepts and principles prevalent in Black vernacular forms and histories. Study of important and innovative jazz dancers, choreographers, and legacies within the jazz dance continuum. Material engagement modalities will include readings, lectures, discussions, films/video clips, and creative assignments.","cross_listed":["DANCE 264"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-264","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"227","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"227","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"227","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Nikolas C. Owens"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 265W","title":"Racism & Anti-racism","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Our present age demands we explore the theory and practice of anti-racist work through an examination of the foundational construction of race and racism in America and the resistance of abolitionists like Frederick Douglass, John Brown, and Frances Seward; the role of white supremacy and anti-blackness. This course also seeks to answer questions like: What is anti-racism? Taking a dialectical approach to such questions to explore work by anti-racism scholars and activists like Ibram X. Kendi; the Black Panthers; James Baldwin; Toni Morrison; and recent critical writing on white supremacy.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-265","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"122","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"122","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"122","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Merrill"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 284","title":"Intro Af Am Lit & Theory","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"How do we make sense of the long durée of African American literary history and scholarship from Early Atlantic literature into the present? This course introduces a set of critical concerns across African American literature and theory and meditates on ongoing concerns that are core to the discipline of Black thought. Students will read interdisciplinarily and across a variety of time periods around concepts of canon and genre, identity and consciousness, music and expressivity, and representation and realism. Thinkers will include: Phillis Wheatley, Frederick Douglass, Richard Wright, Zora Neale Hurston, Ralph Ellison, Gwendolyn Brooks, Angela Davis, Hoyt Fuller, Fred Moten, etc. (History or Theory) (SSIH)","cross_listed":["LIT 284"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-284","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"120","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"120","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"120","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 357","title":"Music & the Black Arts Mvmnt","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An examination of the function of music in the Black Arts Movement in the 1960s and 1970s, analyzing how music paralleled literature, drama, and visual art. The course draws connections between earlier forms of Afro-modernism in the Harlem Renaissance and demonstrates how musicians engaged in social activism by protesting systemic racism and white supremacy. The course considers Larry Neal's theory of the Black Aesthetic and Robert L. Douglas' theory of multidominant elements as lenses for interdisciplinary analysis. Includes a study of musicians who engaged in political activism during the Black Arts Movement and the Movement's influence on early hip-hop artists such as Public Enemy and Poor Righteous Teachers.","cross_listed":["AFRST 257","MUSIC 257","MUSIC 357"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
