{"code":"AMST 117","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as Coal Miner's Daughter, Nashville, and O Brother, Where Art Thou? No previous knowledge of music required. AMST-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 217","MUSIC 117","MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Soph, and 1st Yrs.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 128","title":"Indigenous Lifeways","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course explores many of the questions that come to mind when we think about the diverse lifeways of Indigenous Peoples around the world. This course includes perspectives from Native American and Indigenous Studies and a variety of other disciplines.","cross_listed":["AIIS 128"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/100/amst-128","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"202","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"202","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"202","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 203","title":"American Pilgrimages","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course explores journeys to a variety of destinations in and beyond the Americas, and the transformation and reorientation that often happens along the way for pilgrims of diverse American communities. Topics include Native American spatial practices in the Southwest, among others.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/200/amst-203","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"G041","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"G041","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"G041","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 217","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as Coal Miner's Daughter, Nashville, and O Brother, Where Art Thou? No previous knowledge of music required. AMST-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Soph, and 1st Yrs. Instructor's signature required.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 283","title":"Intro Asian-amer Lit","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examination of themes, forms, and history of literary production by people of Asian descent in the United States. We will survey translated and English-language works by Asian American writers of varying ethnic affiliations, including Chinese, Japanese, Filipino, Korean, Indian, and others. We'll explore how each writer negotiates a relationship with a particular cultural heritage, as well as confronts the racial, cultural, and political formations of the U.S.. Authors include Maxine Hong Kingston, Carlos Bulosan, John Okada, the Angel Island poets, and others. Prerequisites, One course in literature. (Theory or History or SSIH).","cross_listed":["LIT 283"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/200/amst-283","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"227","types":["Asian Studies","Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., and Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"227","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"227","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Steven G. Yao"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 550","title":"Honors Thesis","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Independent study required for honors candidates, culminating in a thesis. Registration only by express approval of the program director.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/500/amst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 113","title":"Prin Soc & Cultural Anthr","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Cross-cultural approaches to the study of such topics as inequality, polity, language, economic behavior, the body, and other categorical distinctions emergent from human practice. Exposure to anthropological theory, methods, and ethnography. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/100/anthr-113","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"201","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Spencer C. Chen"]},{"days":"TR","room":"105","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 207W","title":"The University","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Just what is a university, understood anthropologically? How do we understand its many subcultures, its organizational forms, its economies and its ideological functions? In this course, we will critically examine these questions, and discuss how \"the university\" is shaped by forces of globalization, migration, neoliberalism, and discourses of diversity. We will read ethnographies about \"the university\", analyzing how \"the university,\" as a cultured institution, produces and reproduces various forms of inequality as well as generates new forms of subjectivity. May be taught as an FYC at the discretion of the department. No prerequisites when taught as an FYC.","cross_listed":["EDUC 207W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/200/anthr-207","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"250","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First-Years Only. (FYC)","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Chenyu Wang"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 264W","title":"Ethnogrphy Lit&visual Lang","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Theory and analysis of communication and meaning in social and cultural context with particular attention devoted to the often-neglected aspects of literate communication.","cross_listed":["EDUC 264W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/200/anthr-264","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"127","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"127","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"127","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 315","title":"Lavender Anthropology","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"This advanced seminar examines the anthropological study of sexuality, gender diversity, and queer lives through an interdisciplinary lens, engaging scholarship from queer studies, gender studies, sociology, psychology, and philosophy. Rather than treating sexuality and gender as fixed identities, the course emphasizes ethnographic and comparative approaches that attend to social practice, power, and historical and political context. Students develop advanced analytic tools for critically examining how sexuality and gender are produced, regulated, and contested across cultural settings. Topics include the emergence of sexuality as an analytic category; queer and trans epistemologies; intersectionality; regimes of normativity; intimacy, embodiment, and sexual economies; activism; and the politics of visibility and recognition.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-315","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"124","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"124","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Spencer C. Chen"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 329","title":"Globalization and Education","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"While human communities have always been connected to one another in important ways, recent history has seen a quickening of transportation and communication, increasing the circulation of people, objects, and forms across significant distances. What are the effects of such circulation, for whom, and in what geographies? How does such circulation shaped education around the world? We will examine several \"problem-spaces\" relevant to the study of education, such as globalization and race, immigration, global testing and assessment regimes, education and human rights, etc.","cross_listed":["EDUC 329"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-329","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"142","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chenyu Wang"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 500","title":"Sr Sem Cultural Anthr","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"The research process as it relates to the fulfillment of the senior project, including the formulation of a research problem, frames for research, research design, collection of data and cultural analysis.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/500/anthr-500","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"124","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Spencer C. Chen"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 115","title":"First Term Arabic","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"Introduction in speaking, writing, reading and aural comprehension. Textbook readings and exercises with a strong emphasis on interactive verbal production. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities.","cross_listed":["MEIWS 115"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/100/arabc-115","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"142","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 215","title":"Third Term Arabic","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"Intermediate level study with emphasis on verbal proficiency, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities. Short readings from authentic sources.","cross_listed":["MEIWS 215"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/200/arabc-215","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"210","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 106","title":"Principles of Archaeology","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the fundamentals of archaeology, with emphasis on human biological and cultural records. Topics include a review of archaeological field methods such as sampling, survey and excavation, and analytic methods such as dating, typology and formation processes. Three hours of class with lab exercises embedded within that time. Occasionally two sections of this course are offered.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/100/arch-106","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"2012","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"2012","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Eric Hubbard"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"2012","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":"Open to First-Years, Sophomores, & Juniors only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"2012","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Daiana Rivas-Tello"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 252","title":"Archaeology of Food","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"Food is both a biological necessity and a social necessity. Sharing food builds and maintains social relationships, expresses identity, and is often a component of ritual practice. This course uses archaeological methods to study food in the distant and recent past, from the relationship between food and the evolution of our hominin ancestors to contemporary foodways. We'll address major themes in anthropological research on food including: food and human evolution; food acquisition among hunter-gatherer-fisher communities; the inception and spread of agriculture; feasting; and food and identity.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/200/arch-252","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"2012","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"2012","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Daiana Rivas-Tello"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 325W","title":"Analytic Methods Arch","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"A survey of analytic techniques central to archaeological and paleoecological interpretation. Laboratory performance of artifact analysis and classification, computer-aided data management and statistical analysis. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":["GEARC 325W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/300/arch-325","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"2012","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":8,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"2012","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"2012","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Eric Hubbard"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 510","title":"Sr Sem Archaeology","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"Critical evaluation of selected topics in archaeology. Primary research, culminating in a paper for fulfillment of the senior project.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/500/arch-510","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"2012","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Nathan B. Goodale"]}]},
{"code":"ART 104","title":"Drawing","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will introduce drawing as both a tool for generating ideas, and as an art form in itself. Classes will feature experiments designed to spark and sustain creative impulses, as well as more traditional drawing exercises to train the eye and hand. This will be a multidisciplinary approach, focusing less on perfecting a craft than on building communication skills through visual language. Through discussion and critique, students will develop critical skills and learn how to convey their concepts with simple mark-making. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-104","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"208","types":["Proseminar","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"208","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"208","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy Brener"]}]},
{"code":"ART 106","title":"Introduction to Ceramics","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Handbuilding techniques will be employed to explore the sculptural possibilities of clay. A developed visual and conceptual vocabulary will accompany the technical aspects of ceramics through studio practice and class critique. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-106","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"116","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"116","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Rebecca S. Murtaugh"]}]},
{"code":"ART 107","title":"Art & the Environment","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Three-dimensional explorations will focus and reflect upon our relationship with the natural and constructed world. Emphasis on process and the language of materials will direct form, meaning, and the context of objects and installations created. A developed visual and conceptual vocabulary will accompany studio practice and class critique.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-107","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"116","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Rebecca S. Murtaugh"]}]},
{"code":"ART 109","title":"Introduction to Sculpture","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will provide extensive opportunities to develop observational, conceptual and technical skills in sculpture. Basic woodworking, assemblage, casting, carving and installation methods will be covered, while examining the unique demands and benefits of working in three-dimensions. Through lectures, discussions and critiques, students will delve deeply into what it means to make sculpture in the 21st century. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-109","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"116","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"116","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy Brener"]}]},
{"code":"ART 113","title":"Intro to Media:integrated Med","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will serve as an introduction to digital technology in contemporary art making as it relates to interactivity, collaboration, and emerging practices. Emphasis will be placed on hands on experimentation and exploration across disciplines. We will cover installation art, web art, digital and time-based art, and immersive environments. Students will gain an overview of emerging digital practices, collaborative production strategies and correlations to social, aesthetic and theoretical issues. Other topics may include virtuality, performance, cyborgs, and network culture.","cross_listed":["DARTS 113","MUSIC 113"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-113","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"143","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open 1st Years, Sophomores, and Juniors","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"143","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff"]}]},
{"code":"ART 116","title":"Intro to Photography","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Fundamentals of photography through the use of both analog and digital SLR cameras, black and white darkroom techniques, film scanning and digital printing. Emphasis on development of technical and aesthetic skills, and the potential of the medium as a tool for artistic expression. Cameras will be provided by the College. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-116","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"133","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"144","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Robert B. Knight"]},{"days":"F","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"133","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"144","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Robert B. Knight"]}]},
{"code":"ART 160","title":"Figure Drawing","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Application of basic drawing principles to the representation of the human figure, with emphasis on anatomy and proportion. Examination of related topics such as the figure in the environment and portraiture. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-160","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"208","types":["Proseminar","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"208","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"208","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Yulia Levkovich"]}]},
{"code":"ART 203","title":"Introduction to Painting","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the study of the methods and techniques of oil painting, with emphasis on still-life, figures and landscape.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-203","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"207","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"207","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mark B. Alan"]}]},
{"code":"ART 213","title":"Introduction to Video","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to fundamentals of craft and study of experimental video. Emphasis on developing ideas and conveying meaning through video art, working outside commercial traditions. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-213","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"143","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"143","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kerry Downey"]}]},
{"code":"ART 221","title":"Intro to Animation","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the history, tools and language of basic animation styles. This course will trace the history of experiments in animated imagery from 19th Century photography through 20th Century film into 21st Century digital works. The class will cover basic techniques in Photoshop, Adobe Premier, and illustration and animation software interfaces.","cross_listed":["DARTS 221"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-221","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"144","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kerry Downey"]}]},
{"code":"ART 233","title":"Intro to Printmaking","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Exploration of printmaking's interdisciplinary nature, blending drawing and painting via traditional methods including notan, rubbings, monoprint, monotype, suminagashi, linocut, and woodcut. Production of relief prints, carved blocks, print editions and an accordion book. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-233","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"206","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"206","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"206","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mark B. Alan"]}]},
{"code":"ART 240","title":"Inter Med:perf, Ritual & Tech","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This class will cover performance strategies in relationship to digital technology, ritual and the social. We will create collaborative and hybrid work at the intersection of performance, digital media and social practice and map out modes of ritual engagement within these intersections. We will explore \"liveness\" as a digital and/or embodied presence and survey contemporary hybrid performance work. Enrollment 16, No prerequisite.","cross_listed":["DARTS 240","MUSIC 240","THETR 240"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-240","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"143","types":["Co-Lab Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"143","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff"]}]},
{"code":"ART 304","title":"Advanced Painting","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Further exploration of concepts and techniques presented in Introduction to Painting with emphasis on landscape and interiors as subject matter. Reinforcement of oil painting skills and introduction to egg tempera and acrylic. May repeat for credit at increasingly advanced levels with permission of the professor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-304","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"207","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"207","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Yulia Levkovich"]}]},
{"code":"ART 310","title":"Adv Printmaking: Grn Intaglio","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Further exploration of the concepts introduced in Introduction to Printmaking with emphasis on Green Intaglio process. Students will work with safer alternative mordants and materials, and explore a range of non-toxic aquatint techniques. The course emphasizes technically rigorous projects while balancing craftsmanship with environmental responsibility.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-310","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"206","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"206","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"206","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mark B. Alan"]}]},
{"code":"ART 319","title":"Adv Media: Tv Club","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This advanced Digital Media class will take place between the Digital Arts Lab, and the Photo/Video studio. The focus will be on live mediated performance broadcasts, and will address histories of public access television, broadcast media, media collectives, social and activist video art, and social implications of a recent return back to livestreaming via the internet. Additional topics covered will include digital and analog tools for live video processing, interactivity and studio audiences, serial TV and internet show formats, green screen techniques, and narrative strategies that utilize the integration of bodies, music, props and media in real time. The course will include weekly internet live streams of student work to the Hamilton community and beyond.","cross_listed":["DARTS 319","THETR 319"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-319","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"143","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"140","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"143","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff","Emily K. Harrison"]}]},
{"code":"ART 380","title":"Junior Seminar","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Addresses major themes in art criticism and studio practice from 1970 to the present. Students will reflect on these themes through the production of multi media studio projects, classroom discussion, critique, readings, written assignments, field trips, and oral presentation. Prepares art concentrators for independent senior level work through the integration of previous coursework across our curriculum and an evolution of each student's conceptual development. This course requires an additional three hour lab per week.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-380","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"101","types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Restricted to Jr. ART Majors only. Required trip to NYC November 2-4, 2023.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kerry Downey"]}]},
{"code":"ART 501","title":"Senior Project","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"A required one semester course for senior concentrators in which students work closely with a faculty mentor on the development of a cohesive body of artwork. Upon completion of ART-501 with a minimum grade of A- and approval of the Art department faculty, students are expected to enroll in ART-502.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/500/art-501","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"201","types":["Senior Project","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":40,"comments":"Restricted to Art Majors Open to Seniors Concentrators Only","end_time":"19:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"201","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"19:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"16:00"},{"days":"W","room":"205","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"19:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"16:00"}],"start_time":"16:00:00","instructors":["Amy Brener","Mark B. Alan","Rebecca S. Murtaugh","Robert B. Knight"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 100","title":"World Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This course is an introduction to the pre-modern visual cultures of the world. We will focus on themes of identity, mobility, sacrality, and embodiment throughout different historical periods and across cultures. Exploration of these themes will reveal how images, objects, and monuments were made, experienced, and used in relation to social, political, and religious beliefs. Students will also gain a basic understanding of visual vocabulary and literacy.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-100","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"203","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":36,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"203","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"203","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 108W","title":"Divine Body: So & Se Asia","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This class involves the study of the formless divine and the corporeal nature of the body in South and Southeast Asian art. We will examine representations of the Buddha, the Jina, gods and goddesses, queens and kings, and an array of apotropaic motifs in sacred art and architecture from India, Sri Lanka, Cambodia, and Indonesia. Case studies are chosen from a period spanning over two thousand years, beginning with the third century BCE and ending with portrayals of the divine in contemporary art.","cross_listed":["ASNST 108W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-108","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First-Years Only (FYC)","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"108","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 110","title":"Ways of Seeing","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the study of media with a focus on visual media and experience. Students will learn about key moments in media history, from the invention of print to the rise of broadcasting/mass media and the entrenchment of digital/social media. Students will engage with foundational concepts in media and visual studies, and develop tools to critically analyze, discuss, and write about the images that come to us through physical and virtual networks. Topics include: photojournalism, infographics, screen cultures, public relations and advertising, social media, AI, and the effects of digital media on the environment and mental health.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-110","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"236","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 120","title":"Intro Hist & Theory Film","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"A general introduction to the wide world of cinema and cinema studies, focusing on crucial films from many cinematic traditions. Topics include the evolution of film from earlier forms of motion picture, the articulation and exploitation of a narrative language for cinema, the development of typical commercial genres, and the appearance of a variety of forms of critical cinema. Focuses on basic film terminology, with the cinematic apparatus and ongoing theoretical conversation about cinema and its audience.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-120","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 131W","title":"Misfit Objects Islamic World","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Islamic art is often understood as being religious in nature, non-representational, and geographically associated with the Middle East. It can be all these things but also might not be. This introductory level writing intensive class will focus on telling the stories of art objects that are both part of the field of 'Islamic art' and at the same time don't adhere to these stereotypes. Case studies will include objects that are explicitly non-religious, representational, and produced and consumed in locations ranging from Europe to Southeast Asia. Students will have the opportunity to hone their skills writing object stories, with extensive peer and instructor workshopping, revising, and critiquing of written work. The course will also include an experiential component: looking at art objects in museum and special collection settings.","cross_listed":["MDRST 131W","MEIWS 131W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-131","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 211","title":"Make, Touch, Kiss","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Tangible, three-dimensional objects, once made to be held, touched, and even kissed, are far removed from their original contexts in museum settings today. This class is a laboratory in experimentation on making and using pre-modern and early modern art. We will explore the processes of production and reception through hands-on exercises-grinding pigments, playing with parchment, applying gold leaf, and painting polychromy-alongside analyzing primary and secondary sources on objects for devotion. For the final project, students will work in small groups to 3-D model and print an object from museum collections to scale.","cross_listed":["MDRST 211"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 235W","title":"Artists' Writings","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Why do visual artists write? What purposes do their writings serve? The course considers these questions in both historical and current artistic contexts. Beginning with early twentieth-century manifestos and extending to artists' statements, correspondence, and interviews, it examines the vast literature in which artists have discussed their intentions and values and have elaborated, theorized, and contextualized their own work. It also considers language-based conceptual art, where text is presented as the \"art object\" and experimental forms of artists' writings that resist categorization.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-235","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"116","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Mitchell Herrmann"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 236","title":"Visual Culture of Modernity","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This course explores modernity as a revolution in vision and representation. Students will learn about a range of visual media and experiences that emerged between 1850 and 1930, including photography, posters, panoramas, electric lighting, world fairs, and cinema. We will also consider the evolution of painting in light of the larger political, economic, and technological changes of modernity. Our focus will be on Europe and North America in a global context, particularly as we consider how the international circulation of ideas and people resulted in uniquely modern ways of understanding and visualizing the world.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-236","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 276","title":"Building Cairo","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"The historical art and architecture of Cairo and its surrounding lands constitute an immense range of historical periods from the ancient Pharaonic era to the contemporary moment. This course will explore the impact of these and other important monuments upon the urban landscape at large, while also considering its long durée history. Periods covered will include the Pharaonic, Coptic, Early Islamic, Fatimid, Ayyubid, Mamluk, Ottoman, 19th Century, the Arab Republic, and the contemporary period. The class will be connecting these rich artistic and architectural remains with various historical contexts including the fashioning of rulership and authority, religious patronage, gender, sexual, and racial identities, travel and commerce, 19th and 20th century modernities, political action and activism, among others.","cross_listed":["MEIWS 276"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-276","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"202","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"202","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"202","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 290","title":"Hist Documentary Cinema","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"The history of cinema as representation and interpretation of \"reality,\" focusing on nonfiction film and video from a variety of periods and geographic locales. Emphasis on the ways in which nonfiction films can subvert viewers'' conventional expectations and their personal security. Forms to be discussed include the city symphony, ethnographic documentary, propaganda, nature film, direct cinema, cinma vrit, the compilation film and personal documentary.","cross_listed":["CNMS 290","LIT 290"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-290","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"125","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"},{"days":"R","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"T","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 296","title":"Global Contemporary Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Global Contemporary Art surveys the expanding definitions, contexts, and forms of art in the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, paying particular attention to the significance of artistic agency, artists' writings, globalization, the art market, and political engagement and social transformation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-296","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"116","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Mitchell Herrmann"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 314","title":"Magazines: Digital Approaches","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"How can digital tools help us understand print visual culture? This research lab-style seminar integrates humanistic inquiry with a collaborative research and project design process integral to STEM fields. Students will read cutting edge work on illustrated magazines including Life, Vogue, and National Geographic and explore digital humanities projects that offer new ways to explore histories of print media. Students will learn about the possibilities and limits of digital tools such as mapping, network visualizations, and text mining, and design their own projects utilizing digitized collections of illustrated magazines.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-314","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"215","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"215","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"215","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 320W","title":"Race & Racism in Middle Ages","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"How can we learn from the pre-modern past to make sense of our current world? This writing-intensive course critically studies the structures and operations of exclusion in the Middle Ages through art and visual culture. Students will develop awareness of the historical instances of discrimination based on race, ethnicity, sexuality, gender, and beliefs in the medieval world. The aims of the course itself are antiracist: we will foster time for thought and self-reflection, generate ideas for implementation, and learn from our readings and writings as well as from each other. Open to juniors and seniors only.","cross_listed":["MDRST 320W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-320","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"116","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 330W","title":"Theories & Methods Art Hist","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"What is art history as a discipline? What do art historians do? How do they go about doing it? Theories & Methods of Art History introduces students to various art historical and interdisciplinary theoretical perspectives and methodological approaches that have shaped the understanding, interpretation, and production of visual art. Course materials consist of primary texts, secondary or applied texts, and artists' texts. Students are thus exposed to theories & methods in their original formulation, in their art historical or practical application, as well as in their adaptation by an artist.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-330","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"108","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 103","title":"Third Term Hindi-urdu","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"This intermediate-level Hindi-Urdu course advances students' ability to communicate effectively in spoken and written contexts. Emphasis is placed on meaningful interaction, comprehension of spoken language, and engagement with short authentic texts. Students will develop greater fluency and comprehension through interactive classroom practice, guided listening and reading, and short assignments based on authentic materials.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-103","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"120","types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"120","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"120","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Corbett Costello"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 108W","title":"Divine Body: So & Se Asia","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"This class involves the study of the formless divine and the corporeal nature of the body in South and Southeast Asian art. We will examine representations of the Buddha, the Jina, gods and goddesses, queens and kings, and an array of apotropaic motifs in sacred art and architecture from India, Sri Lanka, Cambodia, and Indonesia. Case studies are chosen from a period spanning over two thousand years, beginning with the third century BCE and ending with portrayals of the divine in contemporary art.","cross_listed":["ARTH 108W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-108","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First-Years only (FYC).","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"108","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 116W","title":"Science & Islam:global Hist","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"What is science? Should scientists and historians agree on what science means? How did science in Islamic Asia influence Europe? What role has religious thought and practice played in the history of science? We explore the long term, from the 9th to 19th century, from cosmopolitan Greco-Arabic translation projects, to the rise of European empires; consider change in human-nature relations in the late medieval and early modern human sciences from the Islamic world, including medicine, geography, anthropology, erotology, astronomy. Readings of primary sources in translation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-116","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"123","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"123","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"123","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 118W","title":"Political Islam","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"9/11 catapulted figures such as Osama bin Laden as the face of Political Islam. But there is a longer, complex history behind the relationship between Islam, modern politics, and violence. Through an examination of thinkers such as Sayyid Qutb, Hassan Al-Bana, Zaynab Al-Ghazali, Ayatollah Khomeini, and Abul Ala Maududi, the course will explore their engagement with conceptions of state, nationalism, secularism, democracy, law, gender, and minority rights as they articulated multiple futures for Muslim communities and influenced a variety of political struggles and armed movements. As such, it will think through their intellectual, political, and hermeneutical departures from more traditionalist understandings of Islam and examine the strengths and limitations of their ideas and receptions.","cross_listed":["HIST 118W","MEIWS 118W","RELST 118W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-118","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]},{"days":"TR","room":"250","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 141W","title":"Gurus, Godmen, Godwomen","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"What leads people to place unwavering faith in others? What are the ramifications of relationships between everyday people and those they revere as semi-divine? This course explores issues of faith and charisma within weekly case studies of Indian gurus, popularly referred to as \"godmen\" or \"godwomen.\" We will investigate how such figures gain, use, and sometimes lose popularity and power. The primary context of this course is Hindu India, but we also attend to international gurus such as Swami Vivekananda, Osho, and Maharishi Mahesh Yogi (the Beatles' guru). The final week explores the viability of the \"guru\" category to describe popular US influencers such as Oprah Winfrey and Kanye West.","cross_listed":["RELST 141W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-141","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"203","types":["Experiential Learning","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"203","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"203","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Corbett Costello"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 160","title":"Everyday Islam","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the study of Islam as an everyday lived religion. The course uses interdisciplinary approaches to understanding Muslim beliefs, practices, and institutional practices. Particular focus on questions of revelation, devotion, law, spirituality, and aesthetics. Students develop facility with analyzing Islamic texts and material culture.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-160","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"005","types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"005","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"005","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 303","title":"Corp Buddhism, Money, Kings","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Buddhism in the modern world is often presented as a philosophical religion that promotes spirituality, monasticism, and meditation in pursuit of enlightenment. But Buddhism also has a complex and fascinating longer history as a corporate institution engaged in trade, land management and commerce. This course explains how monks and nuns engaged with the daily hustle and bustle of economic and political life, and the rise of monasteries as corporate institutions. These institutions became major repositories of wealth and influence and their residents were often sophisticated and successful social and economic actors. Learning about the everyday life of Buddhist monasteries offers insights into today's connections between religious, economic, and political life.","cross_listed":["HIST 303","RELST 303"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/300/asnst-303","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"122","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"122","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"122","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Abhishek S. Amar"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 550","title":"Senior Project","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Seminar in which concentrators develop individually-designed research projects in consultation with the instructor and one other member of the Asian Studies Program Committee. Students discuss their ongoing research with their peers throughout the semester, culminating in formal presentations of the final projects, which demonstrate mastery of interdisciplinary approaches to the study of Asia. Prerequisites, at least one Asian Studies course offered at the 300-level.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/500/asnst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Asian Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"BICHM 321","title":"Physical Chemistry I","department":"Biochemistry","credits":1,"description":"A study of the fundamental concepts and principles of quantum chemistry. Topics include the fundamental postulates of quantum mechanics, the nature of the chemical bond, and applications of molecular quantum mechanics including spectroscopy and computational electronic structure methods.","cross_listed":["CHEM 321"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bichm-biochemistrymolecular-biology/300/bichm-321","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"207","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"207","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Adam W. Van Wynsberghe"]}]},
{"code":"BICHM 346","title":"Biochemistry","department":"Biochemistry","credits":1,"description":"The advanced study of biochemical pathways in living organisms, with emphasis given to gene regulation and metabolism of four major macromolecules: carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids. Includes in-depth discussion of contemporary developments in molecular biology and comprehensive training in molecular techniques. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":["BIO 346"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bichm-biochemistrymolecular-biology/300/bichm-346","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"104","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":30,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Wei-Jen Chang"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"104","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":30,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Wei-Jen Chang"]}]},
