{"code":"BIO 100M","title":"Exb: Cell Rebellion","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"A thematic course exploring five fundamental features of all biological systems, including structure and function, information flow, energy and matter, interactions, and evolution. Cancer arises from the body's own cells gradually acquiring traits that allow them to grow uncontrollably and evade safeguards. In this course, we will investigate how normal cellular processes, such as DNA replication, gene expression, metabolism, cell signaling, and cell cycle can be distorted during cancer development. We will also explore how the immune system recognizes (and sometimes fails to recognize) cancer cells, and how modern therapies attempt to restore homeostasis.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/100/bio-100m","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G042","types":["Co-Lab Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":30,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G042","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"G042","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Elena Tonc"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 200","title":"Microscopy Fundamentals","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"This course is a broad introduction to many common types of microscopy (bright field, confocal microscopy, electron microscopy). In addition to learning the theoretical foundations, students will also get hands-on practice with microscopes throughout the semester. The course will also cover the basics of digital image acquisition, processing, and analysis. Three hours a week, split between class and lab.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-200","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"2048","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"2048","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"2048","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Kyle S. Martin"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 205","title":"Fundamentals of Neurobio","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the field of neuroscience from a biological perspective. In-depth examination of fundamental concepts in neurobiology designed to introduce students to the electrophysiological, chemical and anatomical features of neurons, brain regions and brain circuits.Investigation of the neurobiological basis of behavior through exploration of topics such as neuronal communication, neuroanatomy, sensory and motor systems, learning, motivation, and behavior disorders.","cross_listed":["NEURO 205","PSYCH 205"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/neuro-neuroscience/200/neuro-205","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"3021","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"3021","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"3021","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Abigail K. Myers"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 210","title":"Ecology & Evolutionary Bio","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"An intermediate course that provides a comprehensive introduction to the fundamental principles of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology, emphasizing their deep interconnectedness. Students will explore the mechanisms of ecology and evolution, spanning from molecular-level changes to population, community, and ecosystem phenomena. Students will develop analytical and quantitative tools to solve real-world ecological and evolutionary problems. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory. Not open to those who took BIO 237. Must also register for BIO 210 lab section.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-210","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"G041","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"G041","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"G041","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Andrea K. Townsend"]}]},
