{"code":"CHNSE 110","title":"First Term Chinese","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"This course is for students with little or no background in Mandarin Chinese. The course covers the first five units of the learning materials, Basic Mandarin Chinese (Speaking & Listening; Reading & Writing). The course will help students develop skills in Mandarin Chinese to conduct basic communication across ethnic, cultural and ideological boundaries and to develop an understanding of Chinese interpersonal behavioral culture. At the end of the course, students will be expected to perform in speaking, listening, reading and writing Chinese at a level of proficiency appropriate for continuing on to the next course in the sequence. Students should also demonstrate a level of cultural understanding suitable for correct performance of assigned tasks in Chinese.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/100/chnse-110","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"TR","room":"309","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Junqing Jia"]},{"days":"MW","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"TR","room":"309","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Junqing Jia"]}]},
{"code":"CHNSE 130","title":"Third Term Chinese","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"Comprehensive review of grammar and development of language skills through communicative teaching. Four hours of class, with additional lab work and individual sessions for each student. Prerequisites, 120 or consent of instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/100/chnse-130","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"111","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Couper Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"111","type":"LEC","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"WF","room":"111","type":"LEC","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Yin Zhang"]}]},
{"code":"CHNSE 160","title":"Chinese-speaking World Film","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"This course examines how films produced across the Chinese-speaking world-including mainland China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore, and immigrant communities-depict diverse Chinese-speaking identities while addressing themes such as cultural heritage, societal change, nationalism, migration, gender dynamics, family structures, class straggles, and generational conflict. The course offers a broad understanding of cinema from the Chinese-speaking world, film theory, cultural studies, and relevant Hollywood films. All films have English subtitles. Requirements include film viewings, presentations, quizzes, class discussions, and a final paper. All lectures and discussions are in English.","cross_listed":["CNMS 160"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/100/chnse-160","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"222","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Mainly designed for First-Years and Sophomores, with limited seats available for Juniors and Seniors.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"222","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"222","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Zhuoyi Wang"]}]},
{"code":"CHNSE 200","title":"Third Year Chinese 1","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"Designed for students who wish to use Chinese beyond everyday conversation. Concentrates on subtleties of Chinese grammar and builds a vocabulary through extensive use of short texts. Includes expository writing. Four hours of class, with additional tutorial and laboratory work. Taught primarily in Chinese.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/200/chnse-200","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"213","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"213","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"213","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Tiao-Guan Huang"]}]},
{"code":"CHNSE 436","title":"Chinese Legends in Film","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"Classic Chinese legends are a lavish source of inspiration for contemporary filmmakers. Film adaptations of the legends, including the Chinese domestic ¬box office bonanza Ne Zha and the internationally popular Mulan films, play a significant role in defining popular perceptions of Chinese culture both in and outside China. This seminar engages in an in-depth discussion of the complex and diverse ways in which the legend-based films construct a cultural narrative of China.Requirements include film viewings, presentations, quizzes, class discussions and a final project. Taught in Chinese. All films are subtitled in English.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/400/chnse-436","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"305","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":20,"comments":"Taught in Chinese.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Zhuoyi Wang"]}]},
{"code":"CHNSE 550","title":"Senior Project","department":"Chinese","credits":1,"description":"A research project using sources in Chinese culminating in a paper, designed by the student, in consultation with at least two members of the East Asian Languages and Literatures Department. Students are expected to develop analytical and linguistic skills in the Chinese language through culture study in upper-level coursework and/or study abroad. Limited to senior Chinese concentrators.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/chnse-chinese/500/chnse-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":6,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Zhuoyi Wang","Junqing Jia"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 105","title":"Classical Mythology","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to ancient Greek and Roman mythology, in the context of the wider Mediterranean world, through readings from sources such as Gilgamesh, Egyptian mythology, Homer, Hesiod, Greek tragedy, Herodotus, Livy, Ovid, and contemporary mythmakers. Origins, creation myths, divinities and heroes, and mystery religions. This course is specifically tailored to the First Year Experience but has enough overlap with our CLASC 240 course that students who enroll in this course will not also be able to enroll in CLASC 240.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/100/clasc-105","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"225","types":["First Year Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years only (FYC).","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anne C. Feltovich"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 120","title":"Roman History & Culture","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the history and culture of ancient Rome. Stress on social history and basic skills in the study of history.","cross_listed":["HIST 120"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/100/clasc-120","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"225","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Sunil Persad"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 157W","title":"The New Testament","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"A critical introduction to the literature and history of the Christian New Testament.","cross_listed":["RELST 157W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/100/clasc-157","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"201","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Sunil Persad"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 201","title":"Hist Ancient Western Phil","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"A study of the philosophical classics from early Greek times to the Renaissance. Emphasis on Plato and Aristotle.","cross_listed":["PHIL 201"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/200/clasc-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Justin C. Clark"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 208W","title":"Greek and Roman Medicine","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"A study of the medical theories and practices of ancient Greece and Rome, their later influence, and their relationship with the literature, philosophy and culture of their surrounding societies. We will explore the development of medicine as a discipline in classical antiquity; analyze the evidence that survives for these medical traditions, with particular attention to the Hippocratic Corpus and the work of Galen; and consider points of intersection between issues raised in ancient texts and the concerns of modern medical practitioners.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/200/clasc-208","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"231","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Amy A. Koenig"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 240","title":"Classical Mythology","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"An examination and discussion of the myths of ancient Egypt, Greece and Rome, with particular focus on the reception of these myths in the literature, art, intellectual traditions and social issues of contemporary societies and analysis of how these myths continue to enrich our culture today.","cross_listed":["CLASC 105","RELST 240"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/200/clasc-240","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"305","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Sophia Taborski"]}]},
{"code":"CLASC 286","title":"The Byzantine Empire","department":"Classics","credits":1,"description":"For more than 1000 years following the breakup of the Western Roman Empire, the Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire, with its capital at Constantinople, preserved the legacy of imperial Rome in the medieval Mediterranean. This lecture-discussion course will explore the history of the Byzantine Empire, from the reign of Constantine the Great (ca. 330) to the fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Turks in 1453. Religious, social, and political developments will be considered, along with medieval Greek contributions to the economy and culture of the wider Mediterranean world.","cross_listed":["HIST 286"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/clasc-classical-studies/200/clasc-286","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"202","types":["Medieval & Renaissance St"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"202","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"202","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 110","title":"Ways of Seeing","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the study of media with a focus on visual media and experience. Students will learn about key moments in media history, from the invention of print to the rise of broadcasting/mass media and the entrenchment of digital/social media. Students will engage with foundational concepts in media and visual studies, and develop tools to critically analyze, discuss, and write about the images that come to us through physical and virtual networks. Topics include: photojournalism, infographics, screen cultures, public relations and advertising, social media, AI, and the effects of digital media on the environment and mental health.","cross_listed":["ARTH 110","DARTS 110"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/100/cnms-110","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"236","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 120","title":"Intro Hist & Theory Film","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"A general introduction to the wide world of cinema and cinema studies, focusing on crucial films from many cinematic traditions. Topics include the evolution of film from earlier forms of motion picture, the articulation and exploitation of a narrative language for cinema, the development of typical commercial genres, and the appearance of a variety of forms of critical cinema. Focuses on basic film terminology, with the cinematic apparatus and ongoing theoretical conversation about cinema and its audience.","cross_listed":["ARTH 120","LIT 120"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/100/cnms-120","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 160","title":"Chinese-speaking World Film","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course examines how films produced across the Chinese-speaking world-including mainland China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore, and immigrant communities-depict diverse Chinese-speaking identities while addressing themes such as cultural heritage, societal change, nationalism, migration, gender dynamics, family structures, class straggles, and generational conflict. The course offers a broad understanding of cinema from the Chinese-speaking world, film theory, cultural studies, and relevant Hollywood films. All films have English subtitles. Requirements include film viewings, presentations, quizzes, class discussions, and a final paper. All lectures and discussions in English.","cross_listed":["CHNSE 160"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/100/cnms-160","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"222","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Mainly designed for First-Years and Sophomores, with limited seats available for Juniors and Seniors.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"222","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"222","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Zhuoyi Wang"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 236","title":"Visual Culture of Modernity","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course explores modernity as a revolution in vision and representation. Students will learn about a range of visual media and experiences that emerged between 1850 and 1930, including photography, posters, panoramas, electric lighting, world fairs, and cinema. We will also consider the evolution of painting in light of the larger political, economic, and technological changes of modernity. Our focus will be on Europe and North America in a global context, particularly as we consider how the international circulation of ideas and people resulted in uniquely modern ways of understanding and visualizing the world.","cross_listed":["ARTH 236","HIST 236"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/200/cnms-236","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 239","title":"Japan:a-bomb to Dragon Ball Z","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course delves into the complexities of modern Japan, placing a particular emphasis on pivotal topics such as military conflicts, the pursuit of pacifism, the experience of nuclear victimhood, periods of foreign occupation, and the concept of social responsibility. To achieve a deeper understanding of these themes and to appreciate their varied representations, we employ a multifaceted approach. Our exploration encompasses a range of media forms, including films, radio broadcasts, literary works, and digital archives. Additionally, we will delve into a diverse array of genres, spanning from documentary and war propaganda to personal storytelling. Through these diverse lenses, we aim to cultivate a more nuanced and multifaceted understanding of these historical narratives. Taught in English. No knowledge of Japanese language or history required.","cross_listed":["JAPN 239","LIT 239"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/200/cnms-239","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"207","types":["Asian Studies","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"207","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kyoko Omori"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 268","title":"Political Film","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"Course covers the silent era to the present and examines how films both reflect and influence politics. Topics include the Great Depression, Cold War, politics of race, gender, and class, war films, and the political process. Focus is primarily on American politics, but also includes international films with significant political content.(American Politics)","cross_listed":["GOVT 268"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/200/cnms-268","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"102","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"102","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"102","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Philip A. Klinkner"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 289","title":"Video Essays","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":0.5,"description":"Videographic criticism is a multimodal form of writing. In using sound and image to comment on audiovisual texts, the video essay blurs the line between object and method of study. This hands-on humanities lab introduces students to a wide range of videographic techniques and formats, and some of the theoretical and philosophical concerns driving recent developments in the field. Students learn to not only craft their own video essays, but display greater reflexivity about their aesthetic choices, interventions and compositional process. No prior editing or other digital experience required.","cross_listed":["LIT 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/200/cnms-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"144","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Course runs 8/27/26-10/21/26. 0.5 credit","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"144","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-10-21","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"144","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-10-21","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Pavitra Sundar"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 290","title":"Hist Documentary Cinema","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"The history of cinema as representation and interpretation of \"reality,\" focusing on nonfiction film and video from a variety of periods and geographic locales. Emphasis on the ways in which nonfiction films can subvert viewers'' conventional expectations and their personal security. Forms to be discussed include the city symphony, ethnographic documentary, propaganda, nature film, direct cinema, cinma vrit, the compilation film and personal documentary.","cross_listed":["ARTH 290","LIT 290"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/200/cnms-290","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"125","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"},{"days":"R","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"T","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 304","title":"Religion and Media","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"Investigates the role of various media in shaping religious traditions especially Christianity, Judaism, Islam and Hinduism. Beginning with studies of orality and literacy, we move into the impact of the printing press, then electronic media including Internet and video games. Prerequisites, one course in religious studies or consent of instructor.","cross_listed":["RELST 304"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/300/cnms-304","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"205","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"205","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"205","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["S.B. Rodriguez-Plate"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 314","title":"Magazines: Digital Approaches","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"How can digital tools help us understand print visual culture? This research lab-style seminar integrates humanistic inquiry with a collaborative research and project design process integral to STEM fields. Students will read cutting edge work on illustrated magazines including Life, Vogue, and National Geographic and explore digital humanities projects that offer new ways to explore histories of print media. Students will learn about the possibilities and limits of digital tools such as mapping, network visualizations, and text mining, and design their own projects utilizing digitized collections of illustrated magazines.","cross_listed":["ARTH 314"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/300/cnms-314","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"215","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"215","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"215","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"CNMS 500","title":"Sr Sem Cinema Media Studies","department":"Cinema and Media Studies","credits":1,"description":"Exploration and discussion of topics that relate to both cinema studies and media studies. Students will deepen their understanding through individually-designed research projects, peer reviews and workshops. This seminar will be taught by the director of the Cinema and Media Studies program with collaboration from other faculty in CMS. Students who achieve a grade of 88 or better will be encouraged to pursue an honors project. Prerequisites, Consent of Instructor. (required for Senior Concentrators in the Fall Semester)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cnms-cinema-and-media-studies/500/cnms-500","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"125","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 107","title":"Medical Thinking","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Ethical questions surrounding health and medicine rarely have simple answers: the evidence can be uncertain, knowledge is historically situated, values can conflict, and decisions affect real lives. This first-year course explores how medical reasoning and decision-making depend on drawing from multiple forms of expertise, forms of evidence, and ways of knowing. We will explore human health through a multi-disciplinary lens, including the humanities, social sciences, natural sciences and arts. Students will learn how different disciplines and expertise shape our understanding of health and how we navigate difficult choices made within its context. By examining different roles and perspectives (patients and families, physicians, researchers, policy makers, and innovators), and how these roles have been defined and transformed over time, students will practice communicating across differences and building well-rounded ethical arguments that integrate multiple kinds of knowledge. Through case studies, discussion, and writing, students will build a flexible toolkit for thoughtful leadership in health-related fields.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/100/coleg-107","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"251A","types":["Co-Lab Course","Experiential Learning","First Year Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Fall 2026 Health Lab students only.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"251A","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"251A","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Natalie J. Nannas"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 150W","title":"Academic Communication","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"This course invites first-year students to develop their academic voice through discussion, collaboration, and experience. Through the writing process, students will cultivate a command of academic communication centered around three core skillsets: comprehension of rhetorical strategies, defensible argument development, and analysis of genre features. Students will explore the liberal arts while writing across linguistic boundaries-different languages, dialects, and disciplines. Course discussions will cover topics such as voice, collaborative writing, generative AI, and hypocrisy in academic communication. Writing will be inter-disciplinary and emphasize reflection, research, analysis, and revision as features of effective writing.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/100/coleg-150","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Laura Widman"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 217","title":"Genres Oral Communication","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Oral communication is much more than political speeches or boardroom presentations. This course explores genres of oral communication as they vary across disciplines and contexts, while also considering how technology has impacted the ways in which people express themselves. By examining the development and use of oral communication approaches, students will develop a deeper understanding of the constraints and opportunities offered by various genres of communication. Speaking Intensive.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/200/coleg-217","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"236","types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, & Sophomores Only","end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"236","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy L. Gaffney"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 274W","title":"Justice Lab: Exper & Observ","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Students will work two 3 hour, half-day periods each week Requires discussion, journaling and a research paper that synthesizes various parts of the Justice Lab. Must be taken concurrently with other the Justice Lab courses.","cross_listed":["PPOL 274W","SOC 274W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/200/coleg-274","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"251A","types":["Co-Lab Course","Experiential Learning","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Proseminar","Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Fall 2026 Justice Lab students only.","end_time":"21:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"251A","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"21:30","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"18:30"}],"start_time":"18:30:00","instructors":["Jeremy Rutter"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 395","title":"NYC: Auxiliary/Practicum","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Topic changes each term to reflect the discipline of the director of the Program in New York City. Many resident directors use College 395 as a practicum in which the group takes field trips, or guest speakers join the class, or the class screens documentaries. For a more specific description, see www.hamilton.edu/academics/programs_abroad/nyc/. May include fieldwork. May count toward the concentration in the department or program of the director. Open only to program participants. May be repeated once for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/300/coleg-395","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Patrick D. Reynolds"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 396","title":"NYC: Indep Study","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"An independent study supervised by the director of the Program in New York City and based on an internship and additional research. For a more specific description, see www.hamilton.edu/academics/programs_abroad/nyc/. May count toward the concentration or minor in the department or program of the director. Open only to program participants. May be repeated once for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/300/coleg-396","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Experiential Learning"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Patrick D. Reynolds"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 397","title":"NYC: Internship","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Internship with firm, organization, agency or advocacy group appropriate to the theme of the semester. For more information, see www.hamilton.edu/academics/programs_abroad/nyc/. Does not count toward concentration credit. Open only to program participants. May be repeated once for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/300/coleg-397","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Experiential Learning"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Patrick D. Reynolds"]}]},
{"code":"COLEG 398","title":"NYC: Anchor Seminar","department":"College Courses","credits":1,"description":"Foundational course of the program in New York City, organized around the semester's designated theme. See www.hamilton.edu/academics/programs_abroad/nyc/ for the semester's focus. May count toward concentration credit in the designated departments. Open only to program participants. May be repeated once for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/coleg-college-courses-and-seminars/300/coleg-398","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Patrick D. Reynolds"]}]},
{"code":"CPSCI 101","title":"Computer Science for All","department":"Computer Science","credits":1,"description":"The first course in computer science is an introduction to algorithmic problem-solving using the Python programming language. Topics include primitive data types, mathematical operations, structured programming with conditional and iterative idioms, functional abstraction, and objects. Students apply these skills in writing programs to solve problems in domains across the liberal arts. No previous programming experience necessary. Three hours of lecture and two hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/cpsci-computer-science/100/cpsci-101","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"2048","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"2048","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"2048","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Wenbo Wang"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"G042","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Not open to students taking CPSCI 110 Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G042","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"G042","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_ti