{"code":"ECON 285","title":"Macroeconomic Theory","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Theories of business cycles and economic growth. Theories of monetary policy, budget and trade balances, aggregate consumption and investment activity, unemployment, inflation, technological change and productivity growth.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/200/econ-285","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON majors.","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"T","room":"3024","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Ann Owen"]},{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON majors.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"T","room":"3024","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ann Owen"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 331","title":"International Trade","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Theoretical and empirical analysis of the pattern of international trade and international trade policies. Emphasis on theoretical models used by economists. Topics include the determinants of the pattern of international trade, immigration, foreign direct investment, the gains from trade, tariffs, quotas, voluntary export restraints, dumping, subsidies, trade-related intellectual property rights, international labor standards, trade and environmental issues, the WTO, customs unions, free trade agreements and trade adjustment assistance. Not open to students who are or have taken ECON 433.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-331","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Not Open to Those Who Took ECON 433","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Jeffrey L. Pliskin"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 339","title":"Intro Behavioral Econ","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Rather than assume that people, firms, or leaders are always purely rational, behavioral economics relaxes this assumption and analyzes situations where rationality does not always hold. The field integrates research across disciplines, drawing from neuroscience, behavioral and developmental psychology, and economics. As such it allows economists to study why people choose outcomes that seem to violate traditionally held views of rationality and why markets actually behave as they do. It allows economists to better understand how individuals, groups, and firms respond to policies.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-339","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"102","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"102","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"102","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Case Tatro"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 346","title":"Monetary Policy","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A study of the goals, strategies and tactics of monetary policy. The interaction of the central bank with financial markets, the tools and the transmission mechanism of monetary policy, and structure of the Federal Reserve System and the international financial system. Emphasis on policy application.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-346","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ann Owen"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 417","title":"Topics in Environmen Economic","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"This course covers environmental and natural resource economics topics such as common-pool resources, non-market valuation, carbon prices, environmental justice, and decarbonizing electricity generation. Students will learn to examine the existing literature critically. There will be frequent discussions of journal articles and their policy implications. The focus will be on learning how to use economic theory and statistical methods to formulate and test hypotheses. Prerequisites, 266,275, 285.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-417","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"103","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"103","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"103","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jeffrey R. Cross"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 422","title":"Gender and LGBTQ+ Econ","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"This course will use microeconomic theory, statistical methods, and recent empirical work to examine the causes and consequences of gender and sexual orientation differences in economic outcomes. We will study a variety of topics including education, labor supply, marriage, household, and family choices. This course will 1) examine the history and current state of affairs of gender and sexual orientation differences 2) evaluate policy and its impacts on outcomes.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-422","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"225","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Evelyn E. Skoy"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 437","title":"Topics American Econ History","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the economic history of the United States and its relevance for understanding contemporary policy and economic outcomes. We will study topics such as migration, transportation, education, innovation, industrial development, inequality, and the role of culture. These topics will be situated within major historical periods, including the Westward Expansion, slavery and the Civil War, Reconstruction, the Great Depression and the New Deal, World War I and the interwar years, World War II, and the postwar era. Students will critically read research papers and learn about the empirical methods used in applied microeconomics.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-437","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"103","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"103","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"103","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Jian Qi Tan"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 439","title":"Sustainable Growth","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Is it possible to sustain infinite economic growth on a finite planet? This course uses tools from economics to answer that question. Topics will include the importance of finite inputs in production, whether the externalities of economic growth prevent sustained growth, and climate change. We will study the role that government intervention can or cannot play in promoting sustainable economic growth.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-439","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Hannah Rhodenhiser"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 463","title":"Economics of Hr Mgmt Practice","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Theoretical and empirical study of human resource management including: the choice of the form of labor compensation (e.g. fixed wage, salary, piece rates and other forms of pay for performance); the effects on firm performance of employee involvement programs (e.g., self-directed teams) and of financial participation schemes (e.g., profit sharing and employee stock ownership); hiring; training; promotions; and the level and structure of executive compensation and corporate governance.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-463","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"110","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":19,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"110","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"110","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jeffrey L. Pliskin"]}]},
