{"code":"GOVT 211","title":"Politics in China","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Decline of Confucian China and problems of recreating political order. Topics include rise of the Communist Party, political organization and policy in the People's Republic, role of ideology, foreign relations, the politics of modernization and China's increasing integration into the world economy. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-211","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"127","types":["Asian Studies","Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, & Sophomores","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"127","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"127","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Alexsia T. Chan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 226","title":"Comparative Political Economy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The purpose of this course is to examine the relationship between states and markets. To understand this interaction in theory and practice, we will discuss classic works in political economy and draw on various country case studies. We will also analyze how markets are embedded in institutions, different models of development, and current policy debates on issues such as globalization and inequality. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-226","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Alexsia T. Chan"]},{"days":"TR","room":"109","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Alexsia T. Chan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 251","title":"Intro Public Policy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The study of policy analysis using and comparing a variety of disciplinary and analytic traditions. Consideration of controversies over particular policies at the national and local level and the premises underlying them. Examination of methods and principles used in formulating and evaluating public policy. Open to seniors with consent of instructor. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["PPOL 251"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-251","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"125","types":["American Studies","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Sophomores & Juniors Only","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"125","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Catherine Chen"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 268","title":"Political Film","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Course covers the silent era to the present and examines how films both reflect and influence politics. Topics include the Great Depression, Cold War, politics of race, gender, and class, war films, and the political process. Focus is primarily on American politics, but also includes international films with significant political content. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["CNMS 268"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-268","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"102","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"102","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"102","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Philip A. Klinkner"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 274W","title":"Justice Lab: Exper & Observ","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Students will work two 3 hour, half-day periods each week Requires discussion, journaling and a research paper that synthesizes various parts of the Justice Lab. Must be taken concurrently with other the Justice Lab courses.","cross_listed":["COLEG 274W","PPOL 274W","SOC 274W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-274","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"251A","types":["Co-Lab Course","Experiential Learning","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Proseminar","Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Fall 2026 Justice Lab students only.","end_time":"21:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"251A","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"21:30","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"18:30"}],"start_time":"18:30:00","instructors":["Jeremy Rutter"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 288","title":"Global Prison Reform","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Why does prison reform occur and when does it work? This class explores the politics of prison reform throughout the globe. We will explore cases in which carceral policies shifted in a more punitive direction as well as cases in which reforms attempted to create safer, less crowded, and more humane prisons. We will examine the ways that state actors facilitate or hinder the implementation of prison policies. And, given the course's global focus, will also explore how non state actors like gangs influence the politics of reform and its implementation. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-288","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"236","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Heather A. Sullivan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 290","title":"U.S. Foreign Policy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The major problems of American foreign policy since the republic's founding and the varying approaches U.S. leaders have adopted to cope with American power and principles. Theories are illustrated with detailed examples since WWI. Some attention given to how foreign policy is shaped by government structure, political culture, organizational dynamics, individual psychology, economic interests and other causes. Students will analyze the limitations of various types of explanations and why policy implementation at times diverges from the intentions of decision-makers. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-290","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"125","types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"125","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Alan W. Cafruny"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 321","title":"Federal Internship","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Twelve-week internships in relevant DC office. Interns assume some operational responsibility in the office and gain a perspective on the political and policy process in Washington DC. Does not count toward the concentration. Offered satisfactory/unsatisfactory only. Internship is subject to approval by DC Program Director. Taught in person, unless remote internships can be arranged.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-321","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 323","title":"Intern-observation","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Participants in the program are asked to evaluate their experience in government offices through a series of group discussions and papers focused on particular aspects of the internships. Does not count toward the concentration.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-323","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 325","title":"Sem in Wash Seminar","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"An academic seminar focusing on the public policy process and national issues. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-325","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 327","title":"Sem-wash Indep Resrch","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Preparation and presentation of independent research on a problem related to public policy issues. Use of Washington's unique human and data resources required. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-327","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 329W","title":"Authoritarian Politics","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Consideration of the origins and underpinnings of authoritarian rule. Examines variation in the formal and informal institutions of authoritarian systems as well as the conditions under which transition may take place. Emphasis on critical reading of a growing theoretical and empirical literature in order to gain an understanding the particular problems posed within and by authoritarian regimes. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-329","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"224","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Sharon W. Rivera"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 343W","title":"The Modern State","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"What makes some states weak or strong, stable, or unstable? In this course, we conduct a comparative and historical analysis of the emergence and evolution of the modern state in different regions of the world. We cover a range of topics including the theories of the state, the concept of state capacity and its measurement, the rise and decline of the Welfare State, state-building and its challenges in the developing world, the \"Developmental State\" and its economic achievements in East Asia, and the problem of fragile states in sub-Saharan Africa and the Middle East. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-343","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Pedram Maghsoud-Nia"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 355W","title":"Europe World Affairs","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Examination of European economic and political development and Europe's relations with the rest of the world. Topics include sources of regional economic and political integration; evolution of EU institutions; relations between Europe and the United States; Europe-Russia relations; development of the European monetary system; the crisis of the European social model. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-355","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"104","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Alan W. Cafruny"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 360W","title":"Pol&theory Place & Space","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"How do we map out, conceptualize, inhabit and govern our spatial environment? What political challenges arise in organizing and maintaining a coherent world of places? A look at the theoretical and political dimensions of place and space through writings of geographers, political theorists, environmental thinkers, novelists and U.S. case studies, including 9/11, the debate over logging in the Pacific Northwest, the problem of sprawl, the decline and revival of old industrial cities, the future of America's agricultural landscape, and the impact of climate change. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":["ENVST 360W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-360","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"110","types":["Environmental Studies","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores; open to 1st-years with consent of instructor only.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"110","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"110","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Peter F. Cannavo"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 382W","title":"Public Policy Analysis","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This course helps students understand what public policy analysis is, provides them with a toolkit of practical methods for analysis, and emphasizes professional communication of results in writing. Specifically, it introduces students to cost-benefit analysis and natural-experimental approaches for policy evaluation with real-world examples. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["PPOL 382W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-382","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","types":["American Studies","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Catherine Chen"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 390W","title":"Politics of Silicon Valley","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"In addition to its vast economic and cultural influence, Silicon Valley has become increasingly important to American politics. For example, social media platforms have become major sources of political information (and misinformation); tech billionaires are investing massive amounts of money in American elections and lobbying the government for favorable regulations; various thinkers and intellectuals from or associated with the tech industry are gaining prominence and influence in the broader culture; and, finally, the race for AI supremacy has become a key focus of government policy. This course will examine the distinctive politics of Silicon Valley and its increasing influence on American and global politics.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-390","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"203","types":["Co-Lab Course","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":6,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"203","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"203","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham","Philip A. Klinkner"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 394W","title":"Soc Movements & Pol Protest","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Why do people protest sometimes but more often not? How do social movements work, and why do some succeed while others fail?Examines the origins, dynamics, and consequences of social movements and political protest, exploring both nonviolent and violent movements that have attempted to reshape politics in countries across the globe. Emphasis on critical reading of the theoretical and empirical literature in order to gain an understanding of how political differences across countries shape and are shaped by protest. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-394","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"224","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Heather A. Sullivan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 412","title":"The Politics of A.I.","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This upper-level seminar is structured around three concepts that are central to the politics of AI: algorithms, \"big data,\" and \"humans in the loop.\" Issues discussed include algorithmic discrimination, the state of consent in the age of \"big data,\" and the psychological toll of content moderation on social media. Explicitly interdisciplinary, a variety of methods and academic backgrounds are welcome. The course is speaking intensive. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/400/govt-412","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"119","types":["Co-Lab Course","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"119","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"119","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 419","title":"Topic: Linowitz Sem","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The Sol M. Linowitz seminars are taught by governmental and policy practitioners who have occupied senior positions in government or with global non-government organizations (NGOs). They offer policy-oriented seminars for twelve Government and World Politics concentrators, who are enrolled through a competitive selection process. Professors may be senior diplomats or foreign service officers; state or federal elected officials; senior state or federal cabinet officers or administrative officials; or senior officials of foreign governments, international organizations, or important NGOs. Contact the Government Department for course details and registration information. Permission of the department required. May be retaken for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/400/govt-419","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"104","types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":"Instructor's signature required. Retakeable once, for a total of two instances, both counting toward the major.","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin","Geeta Pasi"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 549","title":"Honors Seminar","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Seminar in which honors candidates in world politics, government and public policy will begin their senior honors thesis. Includes common reading on key issues in political science and research methodology. Requires a GPA of 3.8 in the major (3.8 also for Public Policy) and consent of the department.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/500/govt-549","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"123","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required","end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"123","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Philip A. Klinkner"]},{"days":"F","room":"104","types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Sharon W. Rivera"]}]},
{"code":"GREEK 210","title":"Intermediate Ancient Greek","department":"Greek","credits":1,"description":"Continuation of GREEK-110. Further study of grammar, syntax and vocabulary, accompanied by reading and discussion of passages from classical or New Testament Greek that cast light on ancient Mediterranean society and culture. Students who complete GREEK-210 will be eligible to enroll in any Ancient Greek course at the 300-level. Language course not in English.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/greek-greek/200/greek-210","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"207","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Couper Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"207","type":"LEC","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy A. Koenig"]}]},
{"code":"GREEK 350","title":"The Greek Historians","department":"Greek","credits":1,"description":"The story of ancient Greece as told in the words of the Greeks themselves. Readings, in the original Greek, from Herodotus, Thucydides and Xenophon. Attention to the wider issues of ancient Mediterranean society and culture.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/greek-greek/300/greek-350","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"75","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Anne C. Feltovich"]}]},
{"code":"HEBRW 107","title":"First Term Modern Hebrew","department":"Hebrew","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the Hebrew language introducing the very basic Hebrew grammar, reading, writing and mainly oral communication.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hebrw-hebrew/100/hebrw-107","sections":[{"days":"MF","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MF","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Erella Brown-Sofer"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 100W","title":"Murder, Civil War, Opera","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Ivan the Terrible murdered his heir, and left Russia to face economic collapse and mass hunger without a stable government. Then things got really bad. Did Boris Godunov murder Tsarevich Dmitri? Was the First False Dmitri for real? Only Pushkin knew for sure, but it took Modest Mussorgsky to wrap it up in the greatest Russian opera of all time. This course will explore the relationships between history, art and national identity in Russia.","cross_listed":["RSNST 100W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-100","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"110","types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"110","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"110","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 107W","title":"US Borderlands","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course asks how territorial boundaries have evolved into a dispersed, interior regime of surveillance and control over the course of American history. Beginning with colonial-era treaties and other practices of boundary-making in early US history, we will turn to examine U.S.-Mexico borderlands as a region shaped not only by movement, exchange, and cultural intermingling, but also by the evolving power of the state and the contested processes of border-making. Special emphasis will be given to the intertwined histories of Indigenous, Mexican, Black, Chinese, and Euro-American communities and to examining how borders are produced, negotiated, and lived, while developing critical reading and writing skills in history.","cross_listed":["LTAM 107W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-107","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"103","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"103","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"103","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Nahomi Esquivel"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 110","title":"Intro U.S. Hist 1492-1861","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to U.S. history and the exploration and settlement of British North America, the encounter between Europeans and Native Americans, the colonial era, the American Revolution, the Federalist Era, and 19th-century U.S. history including the growing national division over slavery, concluding with the onset of the Civil War.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-110","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"127","types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"127","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"127","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Douglas M. Ambrose"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 117W","title":"Europe in Photos","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course explores European history through a photographic lens. In the aftermath of the Napoleonic wars, Europeans experienced momentous changes driven by the new politics of liberty and rights, the rise of nationalism, and industrialization. Photography, a new industrial technology and a revolution in sight, enabled Europeans not only to chronicle these changes but also to reconceive time and their place in the world. This course addresses photographs as windows upon the past and as texts and objects requiring historical scrutiny.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-117","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years Only (FYC).","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Kevin Grant"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 118W","title":"Political Islam","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"9/11 catapulted figures such as Osama bin Laden as the face of Political Islam. But there is a longer, complex history behind the relationship between Islam, modern politics, and violence. Through an examination of thinkers such as Sayyid Qutb, Hassan Al-Bana, Zaynab Al-Ghazali, Ayatollah Khomeini, and Abul Ala Maududi, the course will explore their engagement with conceptions of state, nationalism, secularism, democracy, law, gender, and minority rights as they articulated multiple futures for Muslim communities and influenced a variety of political struggles and armed movements. As such, it will think through their intellectual, political, and hermeneutical departures from more traditionalist understandings of Islam and examine the strengths and limitations of their ideas and receptions.","cross_listed":["ASNST 118W","MEIWS 118W","RELST 118W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-118","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]},{"days":"TR","room":"250","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 120","title":"Roman History & Culture","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the history and culture of ancient Rome. Stress on social history and basic skills in the study of history.","cross_listed":["CLASC 120"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-120","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"225","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Sunil Persad"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 134W","title":"Health, Nature, & Discovery","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"How do societies discover cures-and who benefits from those discoveries? This writing seminar examines how medicines derived from the natural world become scientific knowledge, commercial products, or sites of ethical and social debate. Students explore how healing substances move between communities, laboratories, corporations, and governments, raising questions about evidence, expertise, access, and responsibility. Through historical and contemporary case studies, the course addresses traditional and Indigenous medicine, drug development, environmental and public-health impacts, and intellectual property. Writing-intensive assignments build skills in analysis, argumentation, revision, and research across disciplines. No prior scientific or historical background required.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-134","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","types":["Co-Lab Course","First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Fall 2026 Health Lab students only.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 163W","title":"Rise and Fall of Afro Punk","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course will explore the history of the radical politics in African American culture during the twentieth century with a specific focus on music and punk rock. Students will read and learn from a variety of activists, artists, and thinkers as the course explores how Black radicalism in the United States shifted and evolved from the 1960s through the 2000s. This is a writing intensive course that will introduce students to a history of resistance politics by African Americans on the fringes of the mainstream. Students will use the readings to engage these thinkers and historical periods as they develop their own analysis around Black radicalism's impact upon on the U.S. in the past and its legacy in the present.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-163","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"142","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Caleb Smith"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 183","title":"Archive Practice","department":"History","credits":0.5,"description":"Knowledge of best practices and industry standards in the archives field is a valuable life skill with professional and personal applications. Managing the lifecycle of records, determining organization and descriptive schemes, and understanding retrieval systems are tasks undertaken by people in a wide range of capacities. Students will learn these skills through professional instruction and hands-on experiences. They will also be introduced to a viable career field, especially for those interested in working in public history, that combines the arts and sciences. Course is graded S/U.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-183","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"211","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":12,"comments":"Graded Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory. Course runs 10/19/2026-12/18/2026.","end_time":"15:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"211","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:00","start_date":"2026-10-19","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"211","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jeremy R. Katz"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 219","title":"American Jewish History","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course covers significant themes and developments in American Jewish life from the colonial period to the present. Together, we will focus on historic Jewish social, political, religious, and cultural patterns and how they account for the character of American Jewish life today. We will also explore the evolving relationship between Jews and American culture. Finally, we will engage with important historiographic debates in the field of American Jewish Studies. Careful examination of primary sources will further each of these classroom goals.","cross_listed":["JWST 219"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-219","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"205","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"205","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"205","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Benjamin Steiner"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 221","title":"Kievan Rus to Russia","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"A survey of Slavic and Eurasian history from 9th century Kievan Rus' to the Great Reforms of Alexander II. Emphasis on the development of Rus' from scattered principalities to empire. Students will explore possibilities for a new narrative that includes Ukraine.","cross_listed":["RSNST 221"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-221","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"102","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"102","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"102","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 225","title":"Scientific Rev:making Knowled","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Beginning in the Age of Encounters and ending with the Enlightenment, the course traces how the Scientific Revolution fundamentally changed the way we think about nature through key figures, including da Vinci, Copernicus, Kepler, Galilei, Bacon, Descartes, Newton, and Linnaeus. In addition to basic concepts and terminology in the history of science, students will read and analyze primary sources; think critically about how historians write history; evaluate the role of institutions and social customs in the creation of knowledge; and articulate historical arguments orally and in writing.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-225","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"R","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 236","title":"Visual Culture of Modernity","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course explores modernity as a revolution in vision and representation. Students will learn about a range of visual media and experiences that emerged between 1850 and 1930, including photography, posters, panoramas, electric lighting, world fairs, and cinema. We will also consider the evolution of painting in light of the larger political, economic, and technological changes of modernity. Our focus will be on Europe and North America in a global context, particularly as we consider how the international circulation of ideas and people resulted in uniquely modern ways of understanding and visualizing the world.","cross_listed":["ARTH 236","CNMS 236"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-236","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 241","title":"Long Civil Rights Movement","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the political history of the civil rights movement, with a specific focus on the North. This class will be thematically structured and focus on lesser-known events of Black activism in U.S. urban history with a periodization in the twentieth and twenty-first century. Students will analyze the how the civil rights movement in the north is often framed as the \"forgotten\" struggle for Black liberation. They will also explore concepts of historiography, historical memory, and the genesis of the Black protest tradition.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-241","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"142","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"R","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Caleb Smith"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 257","title":"Modern Southeast Asia","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course traces the history of modern Southeast Asia from c.1500 to the early 21st century, with an emphasis on the impact of colonial empires and the emergence of global capitalism. It examines the political, economic, social, and cultural transformations of the region as deeply interconnected with global systems of power and economy. From the \"age of commerce\" through the height of European colonial rule, students will explore how Southeast Asia became central to the expansion of global capitalism. The course also encourages students to engage with historical analysis and critically reflect on the production of knowledge about the region.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-257","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"123","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"123","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"123","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 266","title":"Global Environmental History","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"A survey of topics, themes, and methods in environmental history, focusing on Africa, Asia, and Latin America. Provides a foundation and prepare students for advanced coursework and research in history and environmental studies. Examines major events and trends through an environmental framework, illustrating connections over time. Major course themes include biological exchange between regions, the industrial revolution, climate change, and linking historical environmental issues to contemporary concerns.","cross_listed":["ENVST 266"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-266","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"101","types":["Environmental Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 268","title":"US Labor History","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course traces how the meaning of work in America has transformed from the Industrial Age to the era of influencers and gig labor. Students will learn various methods of historical inquiry to analyze how race, gender, class, and legal status have structured access to work and shaped the possibilities for worker power from the rise of industrial capitalism to the emergence of unions and collective bargaining, the New Deal labor regime, deindustrialization, financialization, and the expansion of service and informal work. We will also study how state intervention has changed the workplace and the economy. With this historical foundation in place, the course turns to the twenty-first century to ask how older labor concepts help us make sense of new forms of work.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-268","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nahomi Esquivel"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 275","title":"Mod. Middle Eastern Hist","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"A survey of the Middle East from Napoleon's invasion of Egypt in 1798 to the present. Examines Muslim responses to European imperialism, political and cultural developments, the impact of the Cold War and the continuing Arab-Israeli rivalry.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-275","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"123","types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"123","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"123","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 280","title":"Imperial China","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Survey of Chinese social, cultural, and political history from the fall of the Han dynasty in the third century to the Manchu conquest in the seventeenth century. Focus on how Chinese writers have understood their own history on such questions of the rise and fall of dynasties and human interactions with powerful cosmic forces that determine fate. Examines the decline of the medieval aristocracy and the emergence of the Confucian gentry; the family and the changing status of women; military conflict with the Mongols and war with Japan in the sixteenth century. No previous knowledge of Asian history required.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-280","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","types":["Asian Studies","Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Thomas A. Wilson"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 286","title":"The Byzantine Empire","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"For more than 1000 years following the breakup of the Western Roman Empire, the Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire, with its capital at Constantinople, preserved the legacy of imperial Rome in the medieval Mediterranean. This lecture-discussion course will explore the history of the Byzantine Empire, from the reign of Constantine the Great (ca. 330) to the fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Turks in 1453. Religious, social, and political developments will be considered, along with medieval Greek contributions to the economy and culture of the wider Mediterranean world.","cross_listed":["CLASC 286"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-286","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"202","types":["Medieval & Renaissance St"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"202","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"202","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 302W","title":"Capitalism: History & Culture","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course is a writing-intensive seminar that examines capitalism as a historical formation, a cultural order, and a system of social thought. It focuses on the emergence, development, and transformation of capitalist societies from the early modern period to the present. Through close reading of influential works by Adam Smith, Karl Marx, Max Weber, Karl Polanyi, Fernand Braudel, E. P. Thompson, Immanuel Wallerstein, and others, the course explores how thinkers and historians have interpreted the rise of commercial society, the expansion of global markets, and the changing organization of labor, wealth, and power. Topics include empire and slavery, class formation, the growth of world economic systems, debates over markets and states, and the social meanings attached to work, risk, and economic life. Alongside these historical questions, the seminar considers how capitalism has shaped everyday practices, moral ideas, and cultural values. Students will engage major texts that have defined the study of capitalism while reflecting on the historical experiences and cultural forms associated with modern economic life.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-302","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"229","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"229","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"229","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 303","title":"Corp Buddhism, Money, Kings","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Buddhism in the modern world is often presented as a philosophical religion that promotes spirituality, monasticism, and meditation in pursuit of enlightenment. But Buddhism also has a complex and fascinating longer history as a corporate institution engaged in trade, land management and commerce. This course explains how monks and nuns engaged with the daily hustle and bustle of economic and political life, and the rise of monasteries as corporate institutions. These institutions became major repositories of wealth and influence and their residents were often sophisticated and successful social and economic actors. Learning about the everyday life of Buddhist monasteries offers insights into today's connections between religious, economic, and political life.","cross_listed":["ASNST 303","RELST 303"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-303","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"122","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"122","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"122","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Abhishek S. Amar"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 319W","title":"Monuments & Myths:civil War","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This research seminar examines the memory of the American Civil War from 1865 until today. As monuments tumble across the country, we will consider how those memorials became such a prominent part of the American landscape. After the war, northern and southern societies, Black and white people, and men and women developed competing visions of the war's meaning. We will look at the memory of the war through film, novels, commemorations, battlefield reenactments, and of course, monuments.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-319","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"142","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ty Seidule"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 377W","title":"Viol,law,justice Middle Ages","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Telling a prisoner that you're going to \"get medieval\" on them does not have positive connotations. At the same time, medieval texts like Magna Carta are held up as having made fundamental contributions to liberal political theory. This research seminar invites students to explore key issues in the development of legal thought and practice in the medieval West that help us understand how various communities and institutions addressed the problem of violence, administered justice, and created social and religious order through law. Prerequisites, WI 100-level History class, History/Lit/MDRST 233, or permission of instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-377","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"124","types":["Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"124","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 394W","title":"Topics:rel Communal Societies","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This research course utilizes the valuable holdings of Burke Library's Communal Societies Collection, this seminar will focus on the various religious communal experiments, especially the Shakers, and their role in American religious history. All students will conduct research in the Communal Societies Collection. May be retaken for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-394","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"213","types":["American Studies","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"213","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"213","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Douglas M. Ambrose","Christian D. Goodwillie"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 396W","title":"Sem History of Gods","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This historiography course offers a comparative study of how gods have been conceived and venerated in early Mediterranean and Asian societies. Students read liturgical texts, hymns and myths to consider the variety of conceptions of gods and the range of ritual forms used to venerate them across the Euro-Asian continent. Draws from theoretical readings to consider such problems as polytheism and monotheism, myth and ritual. Each student shall determine in consultation with the faculty whether his or her written work will focus on historiography or research.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-396","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","types":["Asian Studies","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"231","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Thomas A. Wilson"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 450W","title":"Senior Seminar","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Part one of a sequenced, two-semester course. Students will focus on historiography-the study of historical research and writing-by reading about the discipline of history, engaging with different subfields and methodologies, and thinking about the relationship between history, historical evidence, and the historian. The course culminates with the writing and workshopping of a thesis proposal, which will guide the production of the student's senior thesis in the spring semester.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/400/hist-450","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"224","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley"]},{"days":"F","room":"224","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"224","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kevin Grant"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 110","title":"First Term Spanish","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Introductory Spanish provides a foundation in the Spanish language and Hispanic cultures for students with little or no prior experience studying Spanish. The course emphasizes the development of basic proficiency in speaking, listening, reading, and writing, with a focus on grammatical structures and vocabulary used in everyday contexts. Attention to cultural practices and diverse perspectives across the Spanish-speaking world fosters linguistic and intercultural competence. Meets MWF for 50 minutes plus an additional weekly TA session (50 minutes.)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-110","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"209","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"209","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"209","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 130","title":"Third Term Spanish","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Review of grammar and syntax at the intermediate level, with key emphasis on writing and speaking. Instruction is regularly grounded in guided conversation and communicative practice, allowing students to apply grammatical structures in meaningful contexts. Selected readings and discussion-based activities further develop language skills and deepen students' understanding of cultural practices and perspectives across the Spanish-speaking world. Taught in Spanish. Three hours of class weekly with additional weekly TA session.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-130","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"206","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"206","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"308","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Yelsy Hernandez Zamora"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"206","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"206","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"T","room":"308","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Yelsy Hernandez Zamora"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 150","title":"Fourth Term Spanish","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"An advanced language course designed to strengthen and develop oral communication and writing skills. Conversation and debate play a central role and provide a framework for engaging with cultural topics and contemporary issues in the Spanish-speaking world. The course offers a rigorous review of Spanish grammar and seeks to refine students' written expression through an array of formats including argumentative compositions, descriptive essays, creative narration, newspaper articles, and reviews. Three hours of class weekly, with an additional weekly TA session. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-150","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":"Not open to students who took HSPST-140","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"210","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"210","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":"Not open to students who took HSPST-140.","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"210","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 151","title":"Spanish for Heritage Speakers","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Designed to improve the writing skills of heritage or native speakers with intense emphasis on grammar. The course addresses the specific needs of students who acquired Spanish as a first language, but who have not had extensive formal instruction, seeking to develop writing skills through an array of formats including argumentative compositions, descriptive essays, creative narration, newspaper articles, reviews, among others. Attention will be paid to editing and revision. Students will also refine oral expression through formal presentations and in-class discussion. Three hours of class, with an additional weekly hour of conversation with a student Teaching Assistant. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-151","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":16,"comments":"Not open to students who have taken HSPST 140.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 200W","title":"Exploring Hispanic Texts","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Introductory study of literature and culture from the Hispanic world (Spain, Latin America, and Hispanic United States), with an emphasis on refining oral and written skills in Spanish. Focus on written and oral argumentation, and textual analysis of various literary genres, including narrative, poetry, essay and drama. Additional cultural material studied includes film, art, music, and contemporary social issues. Advanced grammar in context and vocabulary building. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-200","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"List Center","capacity":16,"comments":"Not Open Those Who Took HSPST 201W, HSPST 300, or HSPST majors. Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"231","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"231","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]},{"days":"TR","room":"206","types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":"Not Open Those Who Took HSPST 201W, HSPST 300, or HSPST majors. Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"206","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"206","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jessica N. Burke"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 219","title":"Advanced Grammar Review","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Rigorous review of Spanish grammar at the highest level, including study of the more complex structures relating to the use of the subjunctive,the passive voice and reflexives, the preterite and imperfect tenses, expressions of probability, and the expansion of expository vocabulary. Considerable effort devoted to the development of a mature style of writing, but emphasis placed on the learning and oral practice of grammatical structures. Especially recommended for Spanish majors or minors in their sophomore year, those planning to study abroad or future teachers of Spanish. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-219","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"205","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"205","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"205","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 229","title":"Spanish in the Professions","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course develops communication skills used in business, health, government, law, environmental studies, and social justice. Special emphasis is given to building vocabulary and the improvement of grammatical structures through practical application. Oral and written assignments are designed to expand knowledge of Hispanic social practices while increasing intercultural competence.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-229","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"215","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, & Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"215","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"215","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 250","title":"Journey Span Culture","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"A study of the cultures of Spain, including history, music, painting and other aspects of Spanish civilization which reflect or have contributed to the development of modern Spanish perspectives. Emphasis on contemporary social and political events. Taught in Spanish. Course includes a trip to New York City to see a Spanish play.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-250","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Sr., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Years. Not open to those who took 300. Not open to Senior HSPST majors.","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Juana Sabadell-Nieto"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 320","title":"Others Like Us...or Not","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"The course focuses on women's literature, theory, and cultural production in contemporary Spain. It discusses core topics, issues, conflicts and potential resolutions that shaped and pushed forward Spanish society into a \"less imperfect democracy.\" How does literature and art affect civil rights and social change if they do at all? We will read three representative novels from 1960 to the present, as well as short stories, poems, essays, legal and religious texts, and view films to think about recognition and differences, and, in general terms, about gender. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"309","types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"309","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"309","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Juana Sabadell-Nieto"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 350","title":"The Spanish Inquisition","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"For centuries, the Spanish Inquisition (1478-1834) stood as a powerful instrument of persecution and social control across the Iberian Peninsula and colonial Latin America. This course examines the history and impact of this institution, addressing its medieval precedents and strategies; the construction of heresy and witchcraft; the control of gender, sexuality, race, and the body, as well as censorship and the suppression of knowledge. Course materials include trial records, legal treatises, representations in literature and film, alongside contemporary theoretical studies on state violence and structures of oppression. We explore not only what the Inquisition was, but also what has shaped its legend and whether its logic of repression still endures today. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"213","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Burke Library","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"213","type":"LEC","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"213","type":"FINAL","building":"Burke Library","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Yelsy Hernandez Zamora"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 451","title":"Writers of the Boom","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Studies the narrative of internationally recognized Latin American authors of the 1960's-1970's, contextualizing the literary \"boom\" of this time period, and introducing some of the fundamental works in twentieth century literature. Authors include Carlos Fuentes, Julio Cortzar, Mario Vargas Llosa, Jos Donoso and Gabriel Garca Mrquez. Hispanic Studies concentrators only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Jessica N. Burke"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 110","title":"First Term Italian","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"Introduction in speaking, writing, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with a strong emphasis on obtaining both communicative and cultural competency in Italian. Classes supplemented by online vocabulary-building and grammar practice sessions and Language Center projects and activities. For students with no prior experience in Italian. Those with previous experience with the language will take a placement test in order to be placed at the appropriate level of Italian.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/100/italn-110","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"305","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Valeria Dani"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"305","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Valeria Dani"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 130","title":"Third Term Italian","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"Strengthening of grammar, syntax and conversation skills through interactive communicative activities. Incorporates films, readings and current events to reinforce cultural competency as well as conversation skills. This course is only offered in the Fall. Prerequisites, Italian 110 and 120 or placement.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/100/italn-130","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"305","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"305","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"305","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Valeria Dani"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 145W","title":"City of Women","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"City of Women is a First-Year Course that explores life in modern European cities as experienced by some of the most important female writers of the past century. How does the female perspective challenge a traditionally masculine space that is the city? How does the urban space help women understand themselves? We will focus on the relationship between women and their urban surroundings in a selection of novels, essays, and short stories so as to understand the transformation of gender roles and the modernization of cities from the beginning of the twentieth century to our contemporary world. Taught in English; no knowledge of Italian required.","cross_listed":["WMGST 145W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/100/italn-145","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"209","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First-Years Only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"209","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"209","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 200","title":"Intro Italian Lit & Culture","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"An introductory survey course that offers students continued development of Italian grammar and conversation through literature, film and other cultural products such as music, visual arts and print media. Emphasis on oral and written work. Taught in Italian. Prerequisites, Italian 140 or consent of instructor. Taught in Italian.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/200/italn-200","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"209","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"209","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"209","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 298","title":"Adv Language & Culture","department":"Italian","credits":0.25,"description":"1/4-credit class that must be taken in conjunction with a course in English. Additional weekly session to analyze and discuss the material in the original language. May be repeated for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/200/italn-298","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"JAPN 110","title":"First Term Japanese","department":"Japanese","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to basic structures and vocabulary. Emphasis on oral communication with practice in reading and writing, using the two syllabaries (hiragana and katakana) and 28 kanji characters. Four 50-minute classes per week.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/japn-japanese/100/japn-110","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"TR","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"T","room":"222","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Masaaki Kamiya"]},{"days":"MW","room":"108","types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"TR","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"222","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instru