{"code":"MATH 314W","title":"Real Analysis","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to analysis. Topics include completeness of the real numbers, cardinality, sequences, series, real-valued functions of a real variable, limits, and continuity. Prerequisites, 116 or 216, and 224.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-314","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"105","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Robert Kantrowitz"]},{"days":"MWF","room":"222","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"222","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"222","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Robert Kantrowitz"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 325W","title":"Modern Algebra","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the three fundamental structures of abstract algebra: groups, rings and fields. Prerequisites, 224.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-325","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"225","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"225","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"225","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Courtney Gibbons"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 351","title":"Prob & Stat Inference","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to set-theoretic probability with applications to mathematical statistics. Topics include probability spaces, discrete and continuous random variables, single and multivariate distributions, and limit theorems, leading into the mathematical theory of estimators, sampling distributions, confidence intervals, and hypothesis testing. Prerequisites, Math 216 and an introductory statistics course which may be one of Math 152/252/253, Econ 166, Pysch/Neuro 201, or Govt 230. Students who have a score of 4 or 5 in AP Statistics may enroll with the instructor's permission.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-351","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"308","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"308","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"308","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Clark Bowman"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 508","title":"Sr Sem in Dynamics","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"Various topics from discrete dynamics are explored by working through a series of exploratory modules. Students work in teams and present their findings to the class. Topics include fixed points and their classifications, cycles and their classifications, fractal sets, sensitive dependence and chaos, symbolic dynamics and Sharkovskii's periodic point theorem.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/500/math-508","sections":[{"days":"MF","room":"224","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MF","room":"224","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"308","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Michelle C. LeMasurier"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 513","title":"Sr Sem in Knot Theory","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to knot theory. Topics include classification of different types of knots, the relations between knots and surfaces, and applications of knots to a variety of fields. Prerequisites, Math 325.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/500/math-513","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"309","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"309","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"309","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jose H. Ceniceros"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 514","title":"Sr Sem Optimization","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"Students will work in groups to explore applications of mathematical optimization and their theoretical foundations. Potential topics include signal processing, machine learning, and optimal control, among others.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/500/math","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"308","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"308","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"308","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Erin E. Tripp"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 551","title":"Sr Sem Applied Probability","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An exploration of modern applications of probability theory, including topics such as stochastic approximation, random walks and Brownian motion, graphical models, information theory, and large deviations. Student groups will choose a topic to study in depth and apply probabilistic methods to solve original problems using the statistical programming language R.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/500/math-551","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"101","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"C.A. Johnson Bldg.","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Clark Bowman"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 127W","title":"Medieval Monsters & Marvels","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course will study medieval Britain through its monsters, asking how these aberrant figures manifest the deepest fears and desires of their authors. From Arthurian legends through the world of the Crusades, supernatural figures like fairy lovers, evil giants, loyal werewolves, and cannibal kings negotiate English conceptions of gender, race, and nation. Texts (in modern English translation) may include Beowulf, the Lais of Marie de France, Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, Sir Orfeo, the King of Tars, Mandeville's Travels, and Richard Coeur de Lion.","cross_listed":["LIT 127W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/100/mdrst-127","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"121","types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First-Years Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"121","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"121","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 131W","title":"Misfit Objects Islamic World","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Islamic art is often understood as being religious in nature, non-representational, and geographically associated with the Middle East. It can be all these things but also might not be. This introductory level writing intensive class will focus on telling the stories of art objects that are both part of the field of 'Islamic art' and at the same time don't adhere to these stereotypes. Case studies will include objects that are explicitly non-religious, representational, and produced and consumed in locations ranging from Europe to Southeast Asia. Students will have the opportunity to hone their skills writing object stories, with extensive peer and instructor workshopping, revising, and critiquing of written work. The course will also include an experiential component: looking at art objects in museum and special collection settings.","cross_listed":["ARTH 131W","MEIWS 131W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/100/mdrst-131","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 211","title":"Make, Touch, Kiss","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Tangible, three-dimensional objects, once made to be held, touched, and even kissed, are far removed from their original contexts in museum settings today. This class is a laboratory in experimentation on making and using pre-modern and early modern art. We will explore the processes of production and reception through hands-on exercises-grinding pigments, playing with parchment, applying gold leaf, and painting polychromy-alongside analyzing primary and secondary sources on objects for devotion. For the final project, students will work in small groups to 3-D model and print an object from museum collections to scale.","cross_listed":["ARTH 211"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-211","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"116","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 220","title":"From Chant to Bach","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Solo vocal and choral music of the Medieval, Renaissance, and Baroque eras from Gregorian chant to Bach. Topics include text expression through music, historical approaches to vocal compositional styles and performance practices (a cappella and accompanied), and responses to early music by later composers (Lauridsen, Whitacre, Respighi, Vaughan Williams, Britten, Orff, Prt). Some consideration of the use of early music in film and television (Lord of the Rings, Dangerous Liaisons, Shakespeare in Love, The Adventures of Robin Hood, and Game of Thrones).","cross_listed":["MUSIC 220"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-220","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 222W","title":"Chaucer:gender & Genre","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examines how Chaucer engages and transforms prevailing medieval ideas of gender and genre. Particular emphasis on his constructions of masculinity and femininity in relation to themes of sex, religion, social power and narrative authority. Readings include \"Troilus and Criseyde\" and \"The Canterbury Tales in Middle English,\" as well as select medieval sources and modern criticism.","cross_listed":["LIT 222W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-222","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"218","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"218","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"218","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 225","title":"Scientific Rev:making Knowled","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Beginning in the Age of Encounters and ending with the Enlightenment, the course traces how the Scientific Revolution fundamentally changed the way we think about nature through key figures, including da Vinci, Copernicus, Kepler, Galilei, Bacon, Descartes, Newton, and Linnaeus. In addition to basic concepts and terminology in the history of science, students will read and analyze primary sources; think critically about how historians write history; evaluate the role of institutions and social customs in the creation of knowledge; and articulate historical arguments orally and in writing.","cross_listed":["HIST 225"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-225","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"08:30"},{"days":"R","room":"101","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 227","title":"Shakespeare","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course is about developing the reading strategies to not only understand and enjoy Shakespeare's plays, but to read them in a complicated, critical way. While we will study plays spanning Shakespeare's career, the course will place a special emphasis on reading the plays attuned to how different genres (tragedy, comedy, romance, pastoral, history) are employed and explored. Additionally, we will read significant, complementary material about the culture and the theatre during Shakespeare's lifetime. This course will also make use of the Special Collections.","cross_listed":["LIT 227"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-227","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"124","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Juniors and Seniors only.","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"124","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"124","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Stephanie M. Bahr"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 320W","title":"Race & Racism in Middle Ages","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"How can we learn from the pre-modern past to make sense of our current world? This writing-intensive course critically studies the structures and operations of exclusion in the Middle Ages through art and visual culture. Students will develop awareness of the historical instances of discrimination based on race, ethnicity, sexuality, gender, and beliefs in the medieval world. The aims of the course itself are antiracist: we will foster time for thought and self-reflection, generate ideas for implementation, and learn from our readings and writings as well as from each other. Open to juniors and seniors only.","cross_listed":["ARTH 320W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/300/mdrst-320","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"116","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 332W","title":"Renaissance Drama","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"The course focuses on Renaissance drama, a time when playhouses faced censorship, plague closures and competition from bears wrestling dogs. From being murdered, to prison sentences, to death by plague, many of the writers had a hard time too. We will study plays (some obscure, some quite strange, other rather famous, all enormously interesting) from this very rich and volatile period of literary history.","cross_listed":["LIT 332W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/300/mdrst-332","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"218","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"218","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"218","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Stephen H. Schillinger"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 115","title":"First Term Arabic","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Introduction in speaking, writing, reading and aural comprehension. Textbook readings and exercises with a strong emphasis on interactive verbal production. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities.","cross_listed":["ARABC 115"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/100/meiws-115","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"142","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"142","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"142","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 118W","title":"Political Islam","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"9/11 catapulted figures such as Osama bin Laden as the face of Political Islam. But there is a longer, complex history behind the relationship between Islam, modern politics, and violence. Through an examination of thinkers such as Sayyid Qutb, Hassan Al-Bana, Zaynab Al-Ghazali, Ayatollah Khomeini, and Abul Ala Maududi, the course will explore their engagement with conceptions of state, nationalism, secularism, democracy, law, gender, and minority rights as they articulated multiple futures for Muslim communities and influenced a variety of political struggles and armed movements. As such, it will think through their intellectual, political, and hermeneutical departures from more traditionalist understandings of Islam and examine the strengths and limitations of their ideas and receptions.","cross_listed":["ASNST 118W","HIST 118W","RELST 118W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/100/meiws-118","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"104","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]},{"days":"TR","room":"250","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 131W","title":"Misfit Objects Islamic World","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Islamic art is often understood as being religious in nature, non-representational, and geographically associated with the Middle East. It can be all these things but also might not be. This introductory level writing intensive class will focus on telling the stories of art objects that are both part of the field of 'Islamic art' and at the same time don't adhere to these stereotypes. Case studies will include objects that are explicitly non-religious, representational, and produced and consumed in locations ranging from Europe to Southeast Asia. Students will have the opportunity to hone their skills writing object stories, with extensive peer and instructor workshopping, revising, and critiquing of written work. The course will also include an experiential component: looking at art objects in museum and special collection settings.","cross_listed":["ARTH 131W","MDRST 131W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/100/meiws-131","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"116","type":"FINAL","building":"Art History-Molly Root House","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 215","title":"Third Term Arabic","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Intermediate level study with emphasis on verbal proficiency, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities. Short readings from authentic sources.","cross_listed":["ARABC 215"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-215","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"210","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"M","room":"210","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 276","title":"Building Cairo","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"The historical art and architecture of Cairo and its surrounding lands constitute an immense range of historical periods from the ancient Pharaonic era to the contemporary moment. This course will explore the impact of these and other important monuments upon the urban landscape at large, while also considering its long durée history. Periods covered will include the Pharaonic, Coptic, Early Islamic, Fatimid, Ayyubid, Mamluk, Ottoman, 19th Century, the Arab Republic, and the contemporary period. The class will be connecting these rich artistic and architectural remains with various historical contexts including the fashioning of rulership and authority, religious patronage, gender, sexual, and racial identities, travel and commerce, 19th and 20th century modernities, political action and activism, among others.","cross_listed":["ARTH 276"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-276","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"202","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"202","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"202","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 550","title":"Senior Thesis","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":null,"cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/500/meiws-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 100","title":"Art of Active Listening","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"A study of how to listen to music and get the most out of it. Consideration of approaches to listening to Western art music, film music, jazz, popular music, and selected gamelan and African musics. Focus on strategies of active listening and learning a vocabulary for discussing musical perceptions in various kinds of music. Does not include study of musical notation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-literature-and-history-of-music/100/music-100","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"106","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 110","title":"Intro Music Theory","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"An introductory survey of the most important aspects of Western music theory necessary for performing, interpreting, and composing music. Topics range from reading notes and rhythms to analyzing and formulating chord progressions. Study of basic musical structures of the Western classical tonal idiom (\"classical\" music) and contemporary popular music practices that have developed from that. No previous knowledge of music required. Students who can already read music in both treble and bass clefs, and have a basic knowledge of (1) key and meter signatures, (2) major and minor scales, and (3) simple rhythms should instead register for Music 210.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/100/music-110","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"106","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 113","title":"Intro to Media:integrated Med","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"This course will serve as an introduction to digital technology in contemporary art making as it relates to interactivity, collaboration, and emerging practices. Emphasis will be placed on hands on experimentation and exploration across disciplines. We will cover installation art, web art, digital and time-based art, and immersive environments. Students will gain an overview of emerging digital practices, collaborative production strategies and correlations to social, aesthetic and theoretical issues. Other topics may include virtuality, performance, cyborgs, and network culture.","cross_listed":["ART 113","DARTS 113"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/100/music-113","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"143","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores, and Juniors","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"143","type":"FINAL","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 117","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as \"Coal Miner's Daughter,\" \"Nashville,\" and \"O Brother, Where Art Thou?\" No previous knowledge of music required. MUSIC-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 117","AMST 217","MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, & Soph, and 1st Yrs.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"106","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 125","title":"Applied Music","department":"Music","credits":0.25,"description":"The study of music through lessons in voice, flute, oboe, clarinet, bassoon, saxophone, horn, trumpet, trombone, euphonium, tuba, piano, organ, harp, percussion, classical guitar, jazz/contemporary guitar, folk guitar, violin, viola, cello, and contrabass. Half-hour tutorial for one-quarter credit. Based on evaluation of Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory. A fee is charged. Prerequisites, consent of instructor. Students may repeat courses for credit in Applied Music. Students are encouraged to advance to Solo Performance for further study when they are ready, with consent of the instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/100/music-125","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["John S. Raschella"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"05","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Stephen H. Best"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"10","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Rick J. Balestra"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"17","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Suzanne S. Beevers"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"19","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Michael J. Cirmo"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"25",