{"code":"PHIL 120","title":"Phil Perspective Self","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"What is a self? Does each person have one? Does each person have only one? How is the self related to the soul? Is it unchanging or in constant flux? What is the relationship between the self and the body? Examination of personal identity, the self and the soul as these topics are addressed in traditional philosophical texts, literature and the natural and behavioral sciences.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/100/phil-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"250","types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First-Years and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"250","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"250","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Marianne Janack"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 201","title":"Hist Ancient Western Phil","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A study of the philosophical classics from early Greek times to the Renaissance. Emphasis on Plato and Aristotle.","cross_listed":["CLASC 201"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"105","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"105","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Justin C. Clark"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 224","title":"Knowledge & Education","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A survey of fundamental concepts in education through historical and contemporary texts. We start by thinking about the goals of education, including knowledge, virtue, and skills. Next, we consider human capacities for learning before turning to the nature of learning and various approaches to teaching. Finally, we look at the relation between schools and society and consider how to design more effective institutions for learning. Knowledge and Education is a team-based learning class. Not open to students who have completed Philosophy 124: Philosophy of Education.","cross_listed":["EDUC 224"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-224","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":"Not open to those who took PHIL 124.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"236","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Russell S. Marcus"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 225W","title":"Knowledge & Oppression","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"An examination of the relationship between knowledge and oppression. We explore various ways oppression limits knowledge, as well as how it is that knowledge empowers us to fight against oppression. We consider questions such as: In what ways do forces like racism, sexism, and classism shape what we can come to know? What knowledge does the experience of oppression provide us with? Does ignorance excuse wrongdoing? What knowledge can one have knowledge of oppression they have not experienced? In the future, this course will not be open to students who have completed the new Knowledge and Society course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-225","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"201","types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"F","room":"201","type":"FINAL","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Phyllis Pearson"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 240","title":"Symbolic Logic","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A study of formal systems of reasoning and argument evaluation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-240","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"236","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"R","room":"236","type":"FINAL","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Russell S. Marcus"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 334","title":"Human Nature","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This course will be a survey of the philosophical literature on the idea and definition of human nature.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/300/phil-334","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"207","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Benedict Hall","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"207","type":"LEC","building":"Benedict Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Marianne Janack"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 402","title":"What Is Philosophy?","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This seminar takes up one of philosophy's most unsettling questions - what is philosophy itself? - by centering American philosophy as both a prime site of the question's emergence and a focal point for critical efforts to answer it. Beginning with John Dewey's radical argument that philosophy must abandon its self-image as detached spectator and reconnect with the urgencies of social life, the course traces how that demand was taken up and extended by thinkers working within, against, and beyond the American pragmatist tradition. Over the course of the semester, students will be required to develop and defend a position on what philosophy is, knowing that every answer to that question carries a cost.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-402","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"207","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Couper Hall","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Juniors and Seniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"207","type":"LEC","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"207","type":"FINAL","building":"Couper Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Todd Franklin"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 412","title":"The Politics of A.I.","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This upper-level seminar is structured around three concepts that are central to the politics of AI: algorithms, \"big data,\" and \"humans in the loop.\" Issues discussed include algorithmic discrimination, the state of consent in the age of \"big data,\" and the psychological toll of content moderation on social media. Explicitly interdisciplinary, a variety of methods and academic backgrounds are welcome. The course is speaking intensive. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":["GOVT 412","PPOL 412"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/400/govt-412","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"119","types":["Co-Lab Course","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"119","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"119","type":"FINAL","building":"List Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 417","title":"Seminar: Progress","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This course will give you an opportunity to think carefully about progress. We will read about philosophical conceptions of progress in science, morality, prudence, and philosophy. We will examine popular claims about progress: that AI will revolutionize human societies for the better, that gene selection will allow us to create healthier and happier humans, and that experimentation on animals is worth it because of the intellectual progress it results in. We will ask whether moral and philosophical progress is even possible. And, perhaps most importantly, we will ask practical questions about how to contribute to human progress.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-417","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"205","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Root Hall","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"205","type":"LEC","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"205","type":"FINAL","building":"Root Hall","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Dallas Amico-Korby"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 464","title":"Rorty and His Critics","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"Course will focus on Rorty's work from his early work on philosophy of mind and epistemology through his work on metaphilosophy and politics. We will read not just Rorty's major works, but also some of his critics, including Charles Taylor, Bjorn Ramberg, Linda Alcoff, and John McDowell. Prerequisites, 3 courses in Philosophy or consent of the instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-464","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"R","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Marianne Janack"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 550","title":"Senior Seminar","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"Advanced work aimed at completing a philosophical essay or experiential project on a topic of each student's interest. In collaboration with other members of the seminar, each student will frame and explore a philosophical problem. For a standard project, students will develop an extended thesis-driven essay. For an experiential project, students will develop sustained engagement with activities that develop or apply philosophical views outside traditional academic structures in a reflective, creative, and rigorous way. All projects will be presented publicly at the end of the term. Course cannot be taken Credit/No Credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/500/phil-550","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"109","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":8,"comments":"Restricted to Senior PHIL Majors","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Russell S. Marcus"]},{"days":"F","room":"109","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":8,"comments":"Restricted to PHIL Majors","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"109","type":"FINAL","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":