{"code":"PHYS 200","title":"Physics I","department":"Physics","credits":1,"description":"The first semester of a year-long calculus-based sequence (200-205) for scientists and pre-med students who require a year of physics. Topics include Newtonian mechanics, conservation laws, fluids, kinetic theory and thermodynamics. Three hours of lecture and three hours of laboratory. First year students need instructor's signature to enroll. Prerequisites, Mathematics 116 or equivalent. Not open to students who have taken 100 or 190.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phys-physics/200/phys-200","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G041","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Students who register for PHYS 200 must also register for PHYS 100L. First years by permission only.","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G041","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":"G041","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Theo Jacobson"]}]},
{"code":"PHYS 215","title":"Nuclear Weapons in Wwii","department":"Physics","credits":1,"description":"World War II has been called \"the physicists' war\"; it was perhaps the first global conflict in which physicists played a central role. In this course we examine the scientific paradigms that shaped physicists' effort to understand fission, the relevant fission processes, and the technical principles that went into construction of the first atomic weapons. We also examine the structural and institutional hierarchies that were present at the time of the war, the social disparities involved in the fabrication of the weapons, and the decision to deploy two atomic weapons on Japanese cities.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phys-physics/200/phys-215","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"3024","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"3024","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"3024","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"14:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Katherine A. Brown"]}]},
{"code":"PHYS 290","title":"Quantum Physics","department":"Physics","credits":1,"description":"Wave-particle duality, the nuclear atom, the development of Schrodinger's wave mechanics and the quantum theory of atoms. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phys-physics/200/phys-290","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G047","types":["Proseminar","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"G047","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2026-08-27","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"W","room":"G047","type":"FINAL","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2026-12-18","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2026-12-12","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Seth A. Major"]}]},
